#align(center)[#text(size: 16pt, weight: "bold")[The rational Hodge conjecture for products of K3 surfaces]]
#align(center)[OpenAI]
#align(center)[October 4, 2026]

==== 1 Introduction <sec:introduction>

For a smooth projective complex variety $X$, an algebraic subvariety of codimension $p$ has a rational cohomology class of Hodge type $\( p \, p \)$. The rational Hodge conjecture asks whether these classes span every rational class of that type. Write

$ upright("Hdg")^p \( X \) = H^(2 p) \( X \, bb(Q) \) ∩ H^(p \, p) \( X \, bb(C) \) \, $

where the intersection is taken inside $H^(2 p) \( X \, bb(C) \)$, and write $"CH"^p \( X \)_(bb(Q))$ for codimension-$p$ cycles modulo rational equivalence with rational coefficients.

#thm([Theorem 1.])[
  #emph[Let $m gt.eq 1$, and let $S_1 \, dots.h \, S_m$ be any projective complex K3 surfaces. For $X = S_1 times dots.h.c times S_m$ and every $0 lt.eq p lt.eq 2 m$, the cycle-class map 
$ "cl" : "CH"^p \( X \)_(bb(Q)) arrow.r upright("Hdg")^p \( X \) $

 is surjective.]
] <thm:main>

The factors may repeat, and no relation between their periods or endomorphism fields is assumed. The distinction matters: a product can contain Hodge tensors coupling different transcendental spaces, while a power of one surface can contain tensors beyond those generated by pairwise contractions. The proof treats both phenomena.

For a projective K3 surface $S$, its transcendental space is

$ T \( S \) = "NS" \( S \)_(bb(Q))^perp subset H^2 \( S \, bb(Q) \) \, $

with the intersection form.

===== 1.1 History and the remaining tensor problem <history-and-the-remaining-tensor-problem>

The Hodge conjecture, formulated in Hodge’s 1950 Congress address #cite(label("Hodge1952")), asks which rational cohomology classes come from algebraic cycles. For a projective K3 surface itself, the Lefschetz $\( 1 \, 1 \)$ theorem, together with the classes of the surface and of a point, answers the question. Products introduce a different problem: their Hodge classes include tensors in the transcendental cohomologies of the factors. Already for two factors, the nontrivial part concerns Hodge homomorphisms between these cohomologies; on higher powers, further invariant tensors occur. We use the customary background on K3 surfaces, their periods, and their transcendental Hodge structures as presented in #cite(label("HuybrechtsK3")).

Kuga and Satake associated an abelian variety to a polarized Hodge structure of K3 type #cite(label("KugaSatake")). Its first cohomology carries a Clifford-algebra representation, and the original weight-two Hodge structure embeds into a tensor square of this weight-one structure. Deligne developed this construction in families and used it in his proof of the Weil conjecture for K3 surfaces #cite(label("DeligneK3")) Sections 5–6. André subsequently proved that the Kuga–Satake correspondence is motivated #cite(label("AndreMotives")) Section 7.1. These results provide the Hodge-theoretic and motivic relation to abelian varieties; obtaining an algebraic cycle representing the correspondence is the additional requirement in the Kuga–Satake Hodge conjecture. Van Geemen’s account explains the Clifford construction and its relation to exceptional Hodge classes on abelian varieties #cite(label("VanGeemenKS")).

A second line of work constructs correspondences between K3 surfaces using moduli spaces of sheaves. Mukai’s work supplied the fundamental examples #cite(label("MukaiBundles")), and Nikulin extended the isometry result using quadratic-lattice methods #cite(label("NikulinCorrespondences")). Buskin proved that every rational Hodge isometry between the second cohomologies of two projective K3 surfaces is algebraic #cite(label("Buskin")) Theorem 1.1; Huybrechts gave a proof using twisted derived categories and established an isomorphism of their Chow motives #cite(label("HuybrechtsIsogenous")) Theorems 0.1–0.2. The isometry hypothesis is significant: arbitrary Hodge homomorphisms need not preserve the quadratic forms. Varesco proved that algebraic Kuga–Satake correspondences also make Hodge similarities algebraic #cite(label("VarescoSimilarities")) Theorem 4.5.

Zarhin determined the Hodge groups of transcendental K3 cohomology in terms of its endomorphism field, which is either totally real or CM #cite(label("Zarhin")). This description explains why a result about squares need not settle the problem for powers. In the totally real case, orthogonal invariant theory produces determinant tensors in addition to the quadratic contractions. Varesco identified the corresponding relative determinant classes and proved a precise generation statement #cite(label("VarescoPowers")) Theorem 2.16. When the endomorphism field is CM, the invariants are generated in degree two; the work of Ramón Marí, together with algebraicity of Hodge isometries, gives the Hodge conjecture for all powers #cite(label("RamonMari")) #cite(label("Buskin")); see also #cite(label("VarescoPowers")) Theorem 2.2 and Corollary 2.3. Here a CM endomorphism field is the relevant condition; it need not make the whole transcendental Hodge structure of CM type.

Algebraic Kuga–Satake correspondences have also been constructed for important special families. Paranjape treated the K3 surfaces arising from double covers of the plane branched along six lines #cite(label("Paranjape")), and Schlickewei used their Kuga–Satake varieties to establish the Hodge conjecture for their squares #cite(label("Schlickewei")) Theorem 2. Varesco proved an all-powers theorem for four-dimensional families of generic Picard number 16 with a specified rational transcendental quadratic space, assuming algebraicity of the Kuga–Satake correspondences throughout the family #cite(label("VarescoPowers")) Theorem 0.2. The quadratic-space and family hypotheses in that statement are essential to its scope.

Recent geometric constructions considerably enlarge these cases. Floccari proved algebraicity of the Kuga–Satake correspondence and the Hodge conjecture for all powers of the K3 surfaces associated with varieties of generalized Kummer type #cite(label("FloccariKummer")) Theorem 1.2. Floccari and Fu obtained related results through singular varieties of O’Grady’s six-dimensional deformation type; their construction also gives products of powers of the abelian, K3, and hyper-Kähler varieties associated with one such singular variety #cite(label("FloccariFu")) Theorem 5.7 and Corollary 5.8. These theorems illustrate how geometric relations with abelian varieties control exceptional tensors on particular families. For a general list of K3 surfaces, a further issue is to control the joint Hodge group: algebraicity of the tensors on each individual factor does not by itself determine the mixed invariants.

===== 1.2 The proof and its principal constructions <the-proof-and-its-principal-constructions>

The Kuga–Satake realization of $T \( S \)$ uses the weight-one Hodge structure on the full even Clifford algebra $W_S = C^(+) \( T \( S \) \)$, the $H^1$ of an abelian variety up to isogeny. For a rational vector $w in T \( S \)$ of nonzero square, the standard correspondence is the specified Hodge map

$ T \( S \) \( 1 \) arrow.r "End" \( W_S \) \, #h(2em) v mapsto #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] x mapsto v x w #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . quad "(1)" $ <intro:ks-map>

Here $bb(Q) \( 1 \)$ has weight $- 2$. A polarization converts this into a degree-two tensor on the square of the abelian variety. The precise map in (1) is essential to the argument.

The bridge from geometry to this exact tensor is Proposition 2. It starts with a rational middle-dimensional class killed by the symplectic form on a quartic times a large standard torus, or on the torus alone. Under a condition on its degree-two annihilator, it produces a perfect complex on the ordinary mirror. The conclusion controls two different features of the complex: its Euler pairings with specified test objects and the dimension of its second self-extension space. Part Section 1 first uses this proposition to prove Theorem 1; Part Section 2 then proves the proposition.

The force of the realization statement lies in combining its two conclusions. In the K3 construction, the Euler pairings initially determine only the algebraic component of the complex’s Mukai vector. We choose that component using Clifford multiplication and separated powers of invariant alternating tensors. The Hochschild action on the whole vector factors through $"Ext"^2$, so the small extension space forces many cohomological operators to annihilate it. A Clifford commutant calculation shows that so large a kernel is possible only for the prescribed transcendental component, with its exact normalization. Thus an upper bound on self-extensions determines a tensor that the Euler pairings cannot see.

The same calculation attains equality in the rank bound. Equality makes the semiregularity trace injective, so the complex formally deforms along its Hodge locus. This uses the deformation principle developed by Buchweitz–Flenner and Pridham #cite(label("BuchweitzFlennerSemiregularity")) #cite(label("PridhamSemiregularity")); Markman’s construction of Weil classes by deforming semiregular sheaves gives a related application #cite(label("MarkmanSecant")) Sections 7–9. We apply this principle along the coupled K3 and Kuga–Satake period family. Algebraization and specialization give (1) on every projective K3 surface, as proved in Theorem 8.

This use of an extension-space bound is useful beyond identifying one tensor: it obtains the required Hochschild rank on the algebraic mirror itself. It requires no identification of the Hochschild action under the mirror equivalence. The same method, applied to a torus alone, constructs the degree-four spin tensors of Proposition 12.

Two companion results enter the final algebraic argument. The exact full-even-Clifford criterion of #cite(label("AKS")) Theorem 1.1 gives all Hodge classes on self-powers once (1) is algebraic. The rational Hodge theorem for CM abelian varieties #cite(label("CM")) Theorem 1.1 controls the torus constituents of auxiliary weight-one Hodge structures. These are theorem inputs with the stated scope; the proof below supplies the exact KS correspondences and the additional tensors needed to combine different surfaces.

For the combination, compare the group preserving algebraic correspondences with the joint Hodge group of the transcendental spaces. The self-power results identify the projections of these groups to each factor; the remaining question is whether the relations between different factors agree. Algebraic morphisms between weight-one abelian realizations identify the shared simple factors projectively. Central signs can still differ, especially among the low-rank orthogonal and unitary factors sharing $frak(s l)_2$. The degree-four spin tensors give perfect pairings between local Hom spaces and transport those signs across a finite grid of representation choices. A graph calculation then shows that the remaining signs come from the center of the joint derived group. This is the mixed-product step in Section 5; self-power algebraicity alone does not replace it.

It remains to prove the realization proposition. We represent the input class, after multiplication, by a spin graded Lagrangian immersion whose second cohomology is controlled by the class’s degree-two annihilator. Its self-intersection generators have odd integer degrees. A disk-moduli Stokes identity makes the immersed object unobstructed, and its degree-two endomorphism space is then bounded by that second cohomology. Only the embedded test objects are passed through ordinary mirror symmetry. A directed comparison and a categorical projection produce the perfect complex while retaining the Euler pairings and the extension bound. The geometry adapts the controlled-immersion construction of #cite(label("A8")). Sections 1–4 provide the additional arguments for a K3 factor, arbitrary large torus dimension, integer gradings, the Stokes identity, and the categorical comparisons.

===== 1.3 Conventions and organization <conventions-and-organization>

All cycle classes and correspondences have rational coefficients. An algebraic tensor means a cohomology tensor represented by a rational algebraic correspondence; no assertion about relations modulo rational equivalence is implicit. Hodge groups use the special, or circle, convention and fix Tate structures. In calculations with total cohomology, Tate twists equalizing weights are left implicit only when the circle action makes their meaning unambiguous.

The symplectic constructions use spin branes, zero relative-spin background, and integer Maslov gradings. Novikov fields and their extensions are embedded in $bb(C)$ as abstract fields; these embeddings are not analytic evaluation of a Novikov parameter. The relevant mirror objects are fixed before the rational input class is chosen.

In Part Section 1, Section 1 states the realization proposition and analyzes its mirror tests. Section 2 develops cap-action and deformation tools. Sections 3 and 4 construct algebraic spin tensors; Section 5 uses them to prove Theorem 1. Part Section 2 proves the proposition: Sections 1 and 2 construct the immersion and remove its curvature; Sections 3 and 4 compare the categories.

== 1 Algebraic tensors and the Hodge conjecture <part:algebraic>

==== 1.1 A realization statement and its mirror test objects <sec:setup>

The first step is to realize a rational middle-dimensional class by a perfect complex whose second self-extension group is small. The realization will preserve its pairings with a fixed collection of Lagrangian test objects. We specify those objects here and then use the realization statement to construct the algebraic tensors. Its proof occupies Sections 1–4 in the second part of the paper.

===== 1.1.1 The symplectic spaces and the realization statement <the-symplectic-spaces-and-the-realization-statement>

Let $Y$ be either a smooth quartic K3 surface with the integral symplectic form $omega_Y$ representing its hyperplane class, or a point. In the latter case all $Y$-factors below are omitted. Set

$ T_g = bb(R)^(2 g) \/ bb(Z)^(2 g) \, #h(2em) omega_T = sum_(j = 1)^g q_j and p_j \, #h(2em) D = Y times T_g \, #h(2em) 2 n = dim_(bb(R)) D . quad "(1.1)" $ <setup:spaces>

Here $q_j \, p_j$ denote the coordinate one-forms; we use the same letters for coordinates when describing graphs. Write $omega$ for the product symplectic form. Throughout this construction $g$ is even and $n gt.eq 12$. All branes have integer Maslov gradings, spin structures with zero background class, and the orientations determined by their gradings.

The ordinary mirrors are the mirror quartic $B$ of $Y$ and the product $E^g$, where $E$ is the Tate elliptic curve; set $B$ equal to a point when $Y$ is a point. These varieties are initially defined over Novikov fields. The change of parameter in quartic homological mirror symmetry is included in the definition of $B$. We allow further field extensions and then an abstract field embedding into $bb(C)$. Thus passage to a complex variety means extension of scalars along a field embedding, not evaluation of a Novikov series at a complex number. All fields used below have cardinality at most the continuum.

Choose a finite collection of graded Lagrangian spheres $Sigma_ell subset Y$ which split-generates the quartic Fukaya category and whose classes span

$ P_Y = omega_Y^perp subset H^2 \( Y \, bb(Q) \) . quad "(1.2)" $ <setup:primitive-space>

Their existence is recalled below. For the point use one test object, with the unit convention. In $T_g$ choose finitely many graphs

$ G_M = { p = M q } \, #h(2em) M in "Sym"_g \( bb(Z) \) \, quad "(1.3)" $ <setup:graphs>

and make small Hamiltonian perturbations when transversality is required. The graph gradings vary continuously with the symmetric graph planes; the spin structures come from the base frame. The distinct slopes are totally ordered by positive-definite difference. We include a generating set of scalar slopes and may enlarge the list as described below. This order on slopes is separate from any directed ordering of their Hamiltonian copies.

Denote the mirror images of $Sigma_ell$ and $G_M$ by $Q_ell$ and $N_M$. The graph images are line bundles after one common shift. Put $K_(ell \, M) = Sigma_ell times G_M$. For perfect complexes we use the Euler pairing $chi \( A \, F \) = sum_j \( - 1 \)^j dim "Ext"^j \( A \, F \)$.

#thm([Proposition 1.1.])[
  (Realization with a self-extension bound).  #emph[Fix the spaces and test systems above. Let $a in H^n \( D \, bb(Q) \)$ satisfy $omega smile a = 0$, and define 
$ cal(Z) = { x in H^2 \( D \, bb(Q) \) : x smile a = 0 } . $

 Suppose that cup product induces an injective map 
$ H^1 \( D \, bb(Q) \) ⊗ cal(Z) arrow.r H^3 \( D \, bb(Q) \) . quad "(1.4)" $ <setup:wedge-injective>

 After a field extension and an abstract embedding into $bb(C)$, there is $cal(E) in "Perf" \( B times E^g \)$ such that 
$ dim "Ext"^2 \( cal(E) \, cal(E) \) lt.eq b_2 \( D \) - dim cal(Z) . quad "(1.5)" $ <setup:ext-bound>

 There is a common nonzero $lambda in bb(Q)$, independent of $ell \, M$, with 
$ chi \( Q_ell times.square N_M \, cal(E) \) = lambda thin chevron.l a \, \[ K_(ell \, M) \] chevron.r . quad "(1.6)" $ <setup:euler>

 The mirror varieties, their enhanced categories, and the images of the fixed test systems can be chosen before $a$ is specified.]
] <prop:realization>

The last assertion lets us choose divisor classes and ample classes on the geometric mirror before choosing $a$. One may use a preliminary complex embedding to describe their lattices and cones: the geometric Néron–Severi lattice, intersection pairing, and ampleness of its classes are unchanged by algebraically closed field extension. Later embeddings therefore preserve the algebraic data used in the construction.

The proof of Proposition 2 first constructs a graded Lagrangian immersion representing a multiple of $a$. Its degree-two cohomology gives the right-hand side of (1.5). Unobstructedness places it in a proper cyclic category. We then identify the subcategory generated by the embedded test objects with $"Perf" \( B times E^g \)$ and project to that subcategory. The categorical completion is given in Section 4.

===== 1.1.2 Sphere tests and the algebraic Mukai space <sphere-tests-and-the-algebraic-mukai-space>

In this subsection assume that $Y$ is a quartic K3 surface. Seidel’s quartic theorem gives the enhanced equivalence on a generating category and split-generation by vanishing spheres #cite(label("SeidelQuartic")) Theorem 1.3, Corollary 9.6, and Lemma 11.4. Rescaling the Novikov parameter accommodates the normalization of $omega_Y$. Sphere branes are rational: no one-cycle holonomy condition has to be imposed. Compatibly graded isotopic spheres determine quasi-isomorphic objects by #cite(label("SeidelQuartic")) Lemma 8.14. These facts apply in particular to all the Hamiltonian copies used later.

We may add vanishing spheres from a generic pencil of quartics in $upright(bold(P))^3$. Symplectic transport identifies its smooth fibers with $Y$, and the vanishing cycles span the kernel of ambient inclusion. Their Poincaré duals therefore span $P_Y$. Equivalently one can use a hyperplane pencil in the corresponding Veronese embedding.

For any finite or countable set of the embedded sphere copies, there is a common compatible almost complex structure with no nonconstant holomorphic spheres and no nonconstant holomorphic disks on any individual copy. For somewhere injective curves the unmarked expected dimensions are respectively $- 2$ and $- 1$. Genericity, sphere factorization, and the disk decomposition results of Kwon–Oh and Lazzarini #cite(label("KwonOh")) #cite(label("Lazzarini")) give the assertion, as used in #cite(label("SeidelQuartic")) Section 8. The torus graphs have vanishing relative second homotopy, so a product almost complex structure has the same exclusion property for the embedded product tests.

Write

$ P_B = H^0 \( B \, bb(Q) \) xor "NS" \( B \)_(bb(Q)) xor H^4 \( B \, bb(Q) \) \, quad "(1.7)" $ <setup:algebraic-mukai>

with the Mukai pairing, in which $H^0$ and $H^4$ pair with a minus sign. Before choosing a complex embedding, this denotes the geometric divisor space together with its degree-zero and degree-four generators. For an object $Q$ its Mukai vector is $v \( Q \) = "ch" \( Q \) sqrt("td" \( B \))$.

#thm([Lemma 1.2.])[
  #emph[The space $P_B$ has dimension $21$. Sending the Poincaré dual of a sphere test to the Mukai vector of its mirror image induces a rational isometry 
$ rho : P_Y arrow.r^tilde.op P_B . quad "(1.8)" $ <setup:rho>

]
] <setup:rho-lemma>

#proof[
  The Euler pairing of two sphere branes is minus their intersection pairing. On their mirror objects it is minus the Mukai pairing. Since the test classes span the nondegenerate, $21$-dimensional space $P_Y$, their Mukai vectors span a space of dimension at least $21$.
  The mirror is the geometric generic member of the one-parameter family obtained by resolving the diagonal-group quotient in Seidel’s theorem. That family has nonconstant period. Before taking the finite symplectic quotient, this is visible in the quartic pencil generated by the product of the four coordinates and their fourth powers: at the Fermat member the infinitesimal period in the product direction is nonzero, because $x_0 x_1 x_2 x_3$ survives in the Jacobian ring. The group fixes the holomorphic two-form. Pullback by the quotient correspondence over an open base therefore transports this variation to the resolved family. Its geometric generic Picard rank is at most $19$ by period theory. The HMS parameter is transcendental, including after its formal reparametrization, and geometric Picard rank is unchanged by algebraically closed field extension. Hence $dim P_B lt.eq 21$.
  We have equality. The pairing equality now also proves that the map on sphere classes is well-defined: a relation among their classes maps to a vector orthogonal to a spanning set of the nondegenerate space $P_B$, and thus to zero. The resulting surjective map preserves the pairings, which proves (1.8). ◻
]

===== 1.1.3 Graph images and full faithfulness on the test systems <graph-images-and-full-faithfulness-on-the-test-systems>

We use Abouzaid’s family Floer theorem in its finite-object form #cite(label("AbouzaidFaithful")) Theorem 2.10 and Appendix A. It gives a faithful $A_oo$ functor to analytic perfect complexes. For the present test systems a dimension comparison will give full faithfulness.

The fibration $T_g arrow.r bb(R)^g \/ bb(Z)^g$ is nonsingular, and both its base and total space have vanishing second homotopy. A graph induces $b mapsto \( b \, M b \)$ on fundamental groups, so $pi_2 \( T_g \, G_M \) = 0$. The graphs and their Hamiltonian copies are therefore tautologically unobstructed. The global zero section and the base frame trivialize the section and spin twists in family Floer theory. Let $frak(q)$ be the Tate parameter, of positive valuation. The rigid analytic mirror has multiplicative coordinates $z = \( z_1 \, dots.h \, z_g \)$ and periods $z mapsto frak(q)^b z$, and is $\( E^g \)^(upright("an"))$.

#thm([Lemma 1.3.])[
  (Graph images).  #emph[For each finite test system, the image of $G_M$ is a line bundle up to shift, with numerical type $epsilon M$ for one common sign $epsilon in { 1 \, - 1 }$. After fixing the variance convention, all shifts can be made equal. The family Floer functor is fully faithful on the finite systems and their Hamiltonian copies.]
] <setup:graph-images>

#proof[
  A graph has one transverse intersection with each torus fiber. Its local family Floer complex is consequently a rank-one module in one degree. We recall the numerical calculation of #cite(label("A8")) Lemma 9.1, including its extension to non-diagonal slopes. On the real cover of the base lift a perturbed graph as $p = M q + nabla f \( q \)$, where $f$ is periodic. Comparison paths along this lift have zero character offset: the fixed lattice offset of a lifted graph boundary cancels at its two ends, and a residual fiber loop could not bound. Scalar trivializations on the real cover therefore give the period multiplier
  $ j_M \( b \, z \) = c_b z^(- epsilon M b) \, #h(2em) b in bb(Z)^g \, quad c_b in Lambda^(*) . quad "(1.9)" $ <setup:graph-multiplier>
  Here $Lambda$ is the Novikov coefficient field and a change of variance by perfect duality changes all numerical signs together.
  Every integral symmetric matrix is a sum of signed matrices $u u^t$, with $u in bb(Z)^g$. Products of theta lines pulled back under $E^g arrow.r E$, $\( x_j \) mapsto sum u_j x_j$, have precisely the prescribed type $epsilon M$. Dividing their multiplier into (1.9) leaves a scalar character, hence a line in $"Pic"^0 \( E^g \)$. Projective rigid GAGA algebraizes the image. For the enhanced comparison one can use the common-refinement Čech models of #cite(label("A8")) Section 9; their restriction and cup maps give the same dg comparison in $g$ variables.
  For distinct slopes with definite difference, Floer cohomology has dimension $\| det \( M - M' \) \|$ and is concentrated in one degree. The quotient of the corresponding lines is ample or anti-ample, and its total Ext dimension is the same determinant. Faithfulness is thus fullness for these pairs. For copies of the same slope, self-Floer cohomology has the exterior-algebra dimensions of a $g$-torus. A nontrivial $"Pic"^0$ quotient would have zero cohomology, contradicting faithfulness. The underlying lines of the copies therefore agree, and their self-Ext dimensions again give fullness.
  Choose the arrow direction in which definite graph comparisons have degree zero. The numerical sign must make the corresponding line quotients ample. Otherwise every pair among three ordered scalar slopes would require the same nonzero shift difference $g$, which contradicts additivity of shift differences. With the correct sign, all scalar shifts agree. Comparison with extreme scalar slopes makes the shift of every other graph the same. Grading transport and the self-cohomology support $\[ 0 \, g \]$ give the same conclusion for copies. This is the normalization argument of #cite(label("A8")) Lemma 9.2. ◻
]

===== 1.1.4 Finite generation and the order of choices <finite-generation-and-the-order-of-choices>

#thm([Lemma 1.4.])[
  #emph[Let $H$ be the principal product theta line on $E^g$. There are integers $k_1 < dots.h.c < k_(g + 1)$ such that, for every choice $P_j in "Pic"^0 \( E^g \)$, the lines $H^(- k_j) ⊗ P_j$ split-generate $"Perf" \( E^g \)$. The assertion persists under algebraically closed field extension.]
] <setup:graph-generation>

#proof[
  Choose a vector-bundle generator $V$ of $"Perf" \( E^g \)$ #cite(label("BondalVanDenBergh")). Starting with $K_0 = V$, take evaluation surjections
  $ 0 arrow.r K_j arrow.r W_j ⊗ \( H^(- k_j) ⊗ P_j \) arrow.r K_(j - 1) arrow.r 0 . $
  Here $W_j$ is a finite-dimensional vector space. The exponents can be chosen successively and uniformly in all preceding $P_j$: perform each step over the proper parameter space $\( "Pic"^0 E^g \)^j$, using its Poincaré lines and relative Serre generation and vanishing. The kernels remain vector bundles. The length-$g + 1$ sequence gives a triangle
  $ K_(g + 1) \[ g \] arrow.r C_V arrow.r V arrow.r K_(g + 1) \[ g + 1 \] \, $
  where $C_V$ is a complex of sums of the displayed lines. Since $"Ext"^(g + 1) \( V \, K_(g + 1) \) = 0$, the triangle splits and $V$ is a summand of $C_V$. All evaluation sequences and base-change properties were constructed over the parameter spaces, so the argument persists after field extension. The original generator remains a generator after extension, for example by the usual perfect-diagonal criterion. This is the argument of #cite(label("A8")) Lemma 9.3 in dimension $g$. ◻
]

If the sign convention gives ample rather than anti-ample powers, dualize the preceding construction. Thus the scalar graph tests can always be chosen to include generators. Further tests may be added while retaining definite order: the integral symmetric matrices larger than a fixed matrix in positive-definite order are Zariski dense. Consequently every finite-dimensional span obtained by polynomial evaluation on symmetric matrices can be obtained by continuing the ordered list. Extreme scalar slopes can then be added for the shift normalization.

We fix these finite systems and their mirror images before choosing $a$. Generation in both factors survives the later field extensions. Finally, $E$ is non-CM after every complex embedding used here. Its $j$-invariant has negative Novikov valuation, so is transcendental over $bb(Q)$, whereas a CM elliptic curve has an algebraic $j$-invariant.

==== 1.2 Cohomological operators and deformation <sec:cohomology>

Proposition 2 specifies Euler pairings and bounds an $"Ext"^2$ space. We now express both conclusions in linear algebra. The Euler pairings determine an algebraic component of a Mukai vector; the bound constrains the kernel of the Hochschild cap action on the entire vector. When that kernel has the expected dimension, the semiregularity map is injective, and the complex formally deforms along its Hodge locus. Spreading and specialization then transport the algebraicity of its Chern character. This is the mechanism used in Sections 3 and 4.

===== 1.2.1 The linear dictionary <the-linear-dictionary>

Two gradings will enter the argument. To choose the symplectic input class, we use the Lefschetz action on the cohomology of $D$. To study the resulting complex, we use the actual Hodge grading on the mirror. We first describe the former as a linear model for the latter; identifying their transcendental Hodge structures will require the later rank argument.

We use the Mukai form $q$ on the total even cohomology of a K3 surface: it is the intersection form on $H^2$, and $q \( 1 \, \[ upright("pt") \] \) = - 1$. Mukai vectors are $v \( cal(E) \) = "ch" \( cal(E) \) sqrt("td")$. When using the Hodge circle, we suppress Tate twists; invariance means type $\( p \, p \)$ separately in each cohomological degree.

Suppose first that $Y$ is a quartic. Recall the isometry $rho : P_Y arrow.r P_B$ supplied by the sphere tests in Section 1, where

$ P_Y = omega_Y^perp subset H^2 \( Y \, bb(Q) \) \, #h(2em) P_B = H^0 \( B \, bb(Q) \) xor "NS" \( B \)_(bb(Q)) xor H^4 \( B \, bb(Q) \) . $

The orthogonal complement of $P_Y$ in the Mukai space is

$ U = chevron.l 1 \, omega_Y \, \[ upright("pt") \] chevron.r . $

It is a split ternary quadratic space. Lefschetz theory gives an $frak(s l)_2$-action with Cartan operator $2 - deg$ and lowering operator $f = omega_Y union -$. The space $U$ is its irreducible representation of highest weight two, while $P_Y$ is trivial. Set $t_(+) = 1$ and $t_(-) = - \[ upright("pt") \]$, so $q \( t_(+) \, t_(-) \) = 1$, and choose a weight-zero basis vector $t_0$ of $U$. We identify $"Spin" \( U \)$ with $"SL"_2$. Write $U_B = T \( B \)$. Witt cancellation in the two rational Mukai lattices identifies $U$, as a quadratic space, with $U_B$. This is a rational quadratic identification only; no assertion about the transcendental action of mirror symmetry is used.

For the torus write

$ W = M_0 ⊗ H \, #h(2em) dim M_0 = g \, #h(2em) H = bb(Q) e_(+) xor bb(Q) e_(-) \, #h(2em) f \( e_(+) \) = e_(-) . $

The vectors $e_(+) \, e_(-)$ are the standard $"SL"_2$-basis, of weights $1 \, - 1$. Write $a_i \, b_i$ for the corresponding basis vectors of $W$, $1 lt.eq i lt.eq g$. The creation–annihilation transform is the linear isomorphism

$ Phi : H^(*) \( T_g \, bb(Q) \) arrow.r and.big W quad "(1.10)" $ <coh:transform>

that sends $p_1 dots.h.c p_g$ to $1$, cup product by $q_i$ to exterior multiplication by $b_i$, and cup product by $p_i$ to contraction by $a_i^(*)$. With consistent ordering, the other two creation–annihilation rules are

$ Phi thin iota_(q_i^(*)) thin Phi^(- 1) = iota_(b_i^(*)) \, #h(2em) Phi thin iota_(p_i^(*)) thin Phi^(- 1) = a_i and - . $

Together these four rules and the chosen vacuum characterize the transform. Thus the Cartan operator on the source is $g - deg$, and cup product by $omega_T$ becomes $f$. The transform is not a ring isomorphism.

For a symmetric integral slope matrix $M$, the graph class is, up to a fixed orientation sign, $product_i \( p_i - \( M q \)_i \)$. Consequently

$ Phi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "PD" \[ G_M \] #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = plus.minus exp (sum_(i \, j) M_(i j) a_i b_j) . quad "(1.11)" $ <coh:graph-exponential>

One may verify the identity by the annihilating operators of the graph and its constant coefficient. Mark $H^1 \( E \, bb(Q) \)$ by $H$, and hence $H^1 \( E^g \, bb(Q) \)$ by $W$. The numerical graph calculation in Section 1 makes the right side the Mukai vector of the mirror line, with the common shift understood. The marking is oriented or reverse oriented according to the single sign convention for all graph lines; it is fixed throughout the construction.

The degree-two $"SL"_2$-invariants in $and.big W$ are precisely the expressions $sum M_(i j) a_i b_j$ with $M$ symmetric. The first fundamental theorem for $"SL"_2$ says that they generate its exterior-algebra invariants #cite(label("WeylClassicalGroups")). Expanding the exponentials and polarizing therefore shows that the classes in (1.11) span those invariants. A sufficiently large finite graph list has the same span. Since $E$ is non-CM, these are exactly its power’s rational Hodge classes. Their Euler pairing is nondegenerate and agrees, up to one fixed nonzero scalar, with the intersection pairing under $Phi$: on pairs of graph exponentials this is the determinant formula, and those classes span.

Combine the torus transform with the sphere isometry to define

$ Psi = \( rho xor "id"_U \) ⊗ Phi : H^(*) \( Y \, bb(Q) \) ⊗ H^(*) \( T_g \, bb(Q) \) arrow.r^tilde.op \( P_B xor U \) ⊗ and.big W . quad "(1.12)" $ <coh:dictionary>

Here the target still contains the formal Lefschetz space $U$, rather than $U_B$ with its actual Hodge structure. When $Y$ is a point, set $Psi = Phi$.

We can now state precisely what the Euler tests recover. In the quartic case let

$ C_(*) in \( P_B xor U \) ⊗ and.big W quad "(1.13)" $ <coh:input-space>

be rational and invariant under the simultaneous $"SL"_2$, acting trivially on $P_B$. In the torus-only case take $C_(*) in and.big W$ invariant under $"SL"_2$. Set $a = Psi^(- 1) \( C_(*) \)$. This lies in $H^n \( D \, bb(Q) \)$: total Cartan weight zero means middle degree, and $"SL"_2$-invariance gives $omega a = 0$. If the annihilator of $a$ satisfies (1.4), Proposition 2 gives a perfect complex. Divide its Mukai vector by the common nonzero rational scalar in the resulting Euler comparison, including the fixed pairing normalization of $Psi$, and call the result $C$. Then

$ C \|_(P_B) = C_(*) \|_(P_B) \; #h(2em) upright("in the torus-only case, ") C = C_(*) . quad "(1.14)" $ <coh:determined-component>

Indeed the sphere tests lie in $P_Y$, and the graph tests span all the relevant torus Hodge classes. The $P_B$-component of an algebraic class is itself Hodge. The nondegenerate pairings therefore determine that component. They do not yet determine the component in $T \( B \)$.

===== 1.2.2 The annihilator and cap operators <the-annihilator-and-cap-operators>

The degree-two annihilator of $a$ can be calculated directly on $C_(*)$. A K3 degree-two class becomes $x in P_B xor chevron.l t_0 chevron.r$. Its cup action is

$ D_x \( t_(+) \) = x \, #h(2em) D_x \( y \) = - q \( x \, y \) t_(-) quad \( y in P_B xor chevron.l t_0 chevron.r \) \, #h(2em) D_x \( t_(-) \) = 0 . quad "(1.15)" $ <coh:lowering-k3>

The torus degree-two actions are all operators

$ F \( lambda \, L \, pi \) = lambda and - + F_L + iota_pi \, quad lambda in and.big^2 chevron.l b_i chevron.r \, quad pi in and.big^2 chevron.l a_i^(*) chevron.r \, quad "(1.16)" $ <coh:lowering-torus>

where $F_L$ is the derivation sending the $a$-copy of $M_0$ to the $b$-copy by an arbitrary matrix $L$, and vanishing on the $b$-copy. On the tensor product the full operator is $D_x ⊗ 1 + 1 ⊗ F \( lambda \, L \, pi \)$. These formulas follow from cup product on a surface and the creation–annihilation rules.

The actual Hochschild cap action on $C$ has the same operator formulas, after choosing bases for its Hodge weights. The two calculations have different inputs:

$ & upright("formal calculation") & upright("actual calculation")\
upright("class") & C_(*) & C\
upright("K3 complement") & U & U_B = T \( B \)\
upright("weights") & n - deg upright(" on ") H^(*) \( D \) & p - q upright(" on ") H^(p \, q)\
upright("operators") & H^2 \( D \) upright(" by cup product") & "HH"^2 \( B times E^g \) upright(" by cap product") $

Omit the K3 row and the factor $B$ in the torus-only case. Equality of these operator formulas does not yet identify $C$ with $C_(*)$, or the Hodge circle of $U_B$ with the formal action on $U$. It will let us constrain the unknown component of $C$ using the small $"Ext"^2$ space. We now justify the actual formulas directly on the mirror, without a Hochschild comparison under HMS.

Use Todd-modified Hochschild–Kostant–Rosenberg isomorphisms. On homology the modification wedges with $sqrt("td")$; on cohomology use the corresponding contraction modification. The module theorem of Calaque–Rossi–Van den Bergh identifies cap product with the polyvector action on Hodge cohomology #cite(label("CalaqueRossiVanDenBergh")) Theorem 1.4. It sends the Hochschild class of a perfect complex to its Mukai vector. We take normalized HKR, with the factor $1 \/ p !$ in form degree $p$, and totalized form signs: holomorphic forms have Hochschild homological degree one; antiholomorphic cochains precede polyvector contractions, with the Koszul signs for moving each contraction through a cochain.

For this actual calculation, reuse the weight notation by choosing $t_(+) in H^(2 \, 0) \( B \)$ and $t_(-) in H^(0 \, 2) \( B \)$ with $q \( t_(+) \, t_(-) \) = 1$. Choose $t_0$ in the remaining Hodge line of $U_B$. These are complex Hodge directions, in place of the formal basis $1 \, omega_Y \, - \[ upright("pt") \]$ used above. The weight-zero space is now the full $\( p \, p \)$-part of the Mukai space. The three summands

$ H^2 \( B \, cal(O)_B \) \, #h(2em) H^1 \( B \, T_B \) \, #h(2em) H^0 \( B \, and.big^2 T_B \) $

act as (1.15), with $x$ respectively in $H^4$, $H^(1 \, 1)$, and $H^0$. Here $T_B$ in the displayed groups is the tangent bundle. Wedge product gives the first action; contraction with a tangent-vector-valued one-form gives the middle derivation, skew for the Mukai form; bivector contraction gives the last action. These observations verify the relative signs in (1.15), up to an immaterial rescaling of each acting coordinate. The same form calculus on the abelian factor gives (1.16), now with $a \, b$ the Hodge directions of weights $1 \, - 1$.

There are no cross terms involving the K3 factor, because its $"HH"^1$ vanishes. Consequently

$ dim "HH"^2 \( B times E^g \) = 22 + binom(2 g, 2) = b_2 \( Y times T_g \) \, quad "(1.17)" $ <coh:hh-dimension>

with the term $22$ omitted in the torus-only case. Passing from Dolbeault-normalized to Betti-normalized classes does not alter these rank calculations. In an output component of type $\( j \, j + 2 \)$, an acting polyvector of degree $b in { 0 \, 1 \, 2 }$ uses the Mukai component of half-degree $j + b$. The powers of the comparison unit $plus.minus 2 pi i$ thus amount to invertible row and column rescalings.

#thm([Lemma 1.5.])[
  #emph[Let $cal(E)$ be supplied by Proposition 2, and let $C$ be its normalized Mukai vector. The kernel of the operators (1.15)–(1.16) acting on $C$ has dimension at least $dim cal(Z)$. If it has dimension exactly $dim cal(Z)$, the semiregularity trace on $"Ext"^2 \( cal(E) \, cal(E) \)$ is injective.]
] <coh:rank-trace>

#proof[
  The cap action on the class of an object factors as
  $ "HH"^2 arrow.r "Ext"^2 \( cal(E) \, cal(E) \) arrow.r "HH"_(- 2) . quad "(1.18)" $ <coh:trace-factorization>
  The first map is evaluation of a Hochschild operator on the object; the second is the boundary–bulk trace. Under HKR this trace is the collection of traces against powers of the Atiyah class, followed by an invertible Todd modification. These are the diagonal-adjunction and Atiyah–Chern character identities #cite(label("CaldararuMukaiII")) #cite(label("BuchweitzFlennerHH")). They apply to all the components of the trace, not only to $H^2 \( cal(O) \)$. Rescaling the Mukai vector has no effect on rank.
  By (1.17) and Proposition 2, the composite in (1.18) has rank at most $b_2 \( D \) - dim cal(Z)$. This proves the kernel bound. If equality holds, the composite has rank equal to the upper bound on the dimension of its middle space. Both ranks must then equal that dimension, so the trace out of the middle space is injective. ◻
]

===== 1.2.3 Deforming a complex along its Hodge locus <deforming-a-complex-along-its-hodge-locus>

We use the following precise form of semiregularity. It isolates the only deformation-theoretic input needed after the kernel calculations. The obstruction principle is due to Buchweitz–Flenner; the version for all infinitesimal obstructions and varying ambient varieties is also a consequence of Pridham’s theorem #cite(label("BuchweitzFlennerSemiregularity")) #cite(label("PridhamSemiregularity")).

#thm([Proposition 1.6.])[
  #emph[Let $cal(X) arrow.r S$ be a smooth projective family over a smooth complex algebraic base, let $s in S$, and let $cal(E) in "Perf" \( cal(X)_s \)$. Suppose the parallel transport of $"ch" \( cal(E) \)$ remains Hodge on the germ of $S$ at $s$, and that the map 
$ "Ext"^2 \( cal(E) \, cal(E) \) arrow.r xor.big_(j gt.eq 0) H^(j + 2) \( cal(X)_s \, Omega^j \) \, #h(2em) o mapsto #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Tr" \( o thin "At" \( cal(E) \)^j \) \/ j ! #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]_j $

 is injective. Then $cal(E)$ extends to a compatible family of perfect complexes over the formal germ of $S$ at $s$.]
  #emph[If $S$ is connected and the Chern character is a global parallel section remaining Hodge on $S$, that section is algebraic on every fiber. The same conclusions hold for the Mukai vector in a family whose Todd classes are parallel algebraic classes.]
] <prop:semiregularity>

#proof[
  We first explain the obstruction calculation. Resolve the initial perfect complex by a bounded complex of vector bundles, and use the Dolbeault resolution of its graded smooth bundle. A deformation is represented by a total-degree-one $\( 0 \, * \)$-superconnection squaring to zero. Relative smooth forms may be used over the Artin neighborhoods of $s$: in a smooth local trivialization take Taylor coefficients in base variables and set antiholomorphic base variables to zero. Local holomorphic coordinates identify these complexes with relative Dolbeault and de Rham resolutions. Smooth projectivity gives Hodge degeneration and cohomology base change.
  Consider a small extension of Artin neighborhoods, with kernel $I$ annihilated by the maximal ideal. Lift an already constructed superconnection arbitrarily, retaining the relative Dolbeault Leibniz rule. Its square $o$ is an $I$-valued cocycle in the central endomorphism complex and represents the obstruction. Add a smooth $\( 1 \, 0 \)$-connection and denote the curvature of the resulting full superconnection by $R$. The closed forms $"str" \( R^m \)$ represent the horizontal Chern character, up to the usual nonzero normalization; this follows from the superconnection Chern–Weil transgression formula.
  Sort the curvature by holomorphic form degree. Its degree-zero part is $o$, and the other parts have degrees one and two. Since $I^2 = 0$, every term with two copies of $o$ vanishes. Hence $"str" \( R^m \)$ has no component below holomorphic degree $m - 1$, and its component of degree $m - 1$ is
  $ m thin "str" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] o \( R_0^1 \)^(m - 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, quad "(1.19)" $ <coh:obstruction-trace>
  where $R_0^1$ is the degree-one curvature at the central fiber. It represents the Atiyah class. The horizontal Chern character lies in filtration $F^m$ by hypothesis. Hodge degeneration therefore makes (1.19) zero in Dolbeault cohomology. This remains true with coefficients in $I$ on the central fiber, by freeness and cohomology base change. All semiregularity traces of the obstruction vanish. Injectivity makes $o$ exact, so a correction of the lifted superconnection makes its square zero. Iterating over the powers of the maximal ideal gives compatible lifts.
  These lifts are perfect complexes, not merely formal differential operators. Locally on a coordinate disk, assume inductively that the operator modulo $I$ has been gauged to ordinary Dolbeault plus a holomorphic differential. Lift that gauge and an arbitrary holomorphic matrix lift of the differential. The difference has coefficients in $I$. Its highest positive form-degree component is Dolbeault closed by the square-zero equation. A local primitive gives a gauge transformation removing it. Continue downward in form degree. The remaining square-zero operator is ordinary Dolbeault plus a holomorphic differential. Thus it is locally the Dolbeault resolution of a perfect complex. The relative forms are flat over the Artin base, so the chosen reductions compute derived restriction. GAGA identifies these analytic lifts with algebraic perfect complexes.
  The fixed bounded graded bundle in this local straightening gives a uniform finite Tor amplitude for the compatible lifts. Grothendieck existence algebraizes the resulting pseudocoherent tower over the completed local base #cite(label("PerfectGrothendieckExistence")) Theorem 38.29.6, Tag 0DIG. The family is proper, flat, and finitely presented, and the restriction to the closed fiber is perfect. The relative-perfectness criterion #cite(label("PerfectGrothendieckExistence")) Lemma 37.71.3, Tag 0DJZ therefore makes the algebraization perfect relative to the completed base. That base is regular and the family is smooth, so the total space is regular; the resulting bounded coherent complex is thus perfect. Spread its finite presentation to a finitely generated subalgebra of the completion over an affine neighborhood of $s$, retaining the closed point and its original complex. The resulting finite-type parameter is dominant over that neighborhood: the base is smooth at $s$, and its local ring maps injectively to its completion. On the relevant irreducible component, topological constancy of Chern characters identifies the classes of the spreading complex with the pullback of the prescribed parallel section. Its image contains a dense open subset of $S$.
  For completeness, the last passage to every fiber uses proper cycle parameters, not constancy of a chosen complex. Express algebraicity of the fixed rational parallel class by finite sums and differences of cycles with a common positive denominator. Relative projective Hilbert schemes give countably many proper parameter spaces for such expressions; restrict to connected components on which the cycle class is the prescribed one. Their images in $S$ are closed. A dense open subset of an irreducible complex variety cannot be covered by countably many proper closed subvarieties. One such proper image is therefore all of $S$. This proves algebraicity on every fiber. A connected smooth base is irreducible componentwise with no intersecting components, so the stated connected case follows. Finally, multiplication by $sqrt("td")$ is an invertible parallel algebraic operation in the indicated families and changes none of the conclusions. ◻
]

The remaining task is now finite-dimensional: choose $C_(*)$ so that the annihilator has the required wedge-injectivity, and show that the unknown Mukai component is forced by the cap-kernel bound. Once the bound is sharp, Proposition 7 transports the resulting algebraic tensor through the desired period family.

==== 1.3 Exact Kuga–Satake correspondences <sec:exactks>

We now use the realization and deformation results to construct the prescribed Kuga–Satake map. The distinction between that map and an unspecified nonzero embedding matters: the full Clifford action will subsequently control tensor classes and the actions on different K3 factors.

For a projective K3 surface $S$, put

$ T \( S \) = "NS" \( S \)_(bb(Q))^perp subset H^2 \( S \, bb(Q) \) \, #h(2em) W_S = C^(+) \( T \( S \) \, q \) \, $

where $q$ is the cup form and our Clifford convention is $v^2 = q \( v \, v \)$. Give $W_S$ its standard left Kuga–Satake Hodge structure. Thus, for an oriented orthonormal basis $u_1 \, u_2$ of the positive period plane, its complex structure is left multiplication by $u_1 u_2$. This polarizable weight-one structure is $H^1 \( A_S \, bb(Q) \)$ for an abelian variety, up to the choice of lattice and isogeny #cite(label("KugaSatake")) #cite(label("DeligneK3")) #cite(label("VanGeemenKS")). If $w in T \( S \)$ has nonzero square, the standard tensor is

$ j_w : T \( S \) \( 1 \) arrow.r "End" \( W_S \) \, #h(2em) j_w \( v \) \( x \) = v x w . quad "(1.20)" $ <ks:standard-map>

A rational polarization identifies $W_S^or$ with $W_S \( 1 \)$ and therefore converts (1.20) into a Hodge map $iota_w : T \( S \) arrow.r W_S ⊗ W_S subset H^2 \( A_S^2 \, bb(Q) \)$.

#thm([Theorem 1.7.])[
  #emph[Let $S$ be a smooth projective complex K3 surface. For every nonisotropic $w in T \( S \)$, every full even-Clifford realization $H^1 \( A_S \, bb(Q) \) = C^(+) \( T \( S \) \, q \)$, and every rational polarization used to define $iota_w$, there is a cycle 
$ Gamma in "CH"^2 \( S times A_S^2 \)_(bb(Q)) quad upright("such that") quad Gamma_(*) \|_(T \( S \)) = iota_w . $

]
] <thm:exact-ks>

The proof first constructs this exact map at the mirror quartic $B$. Auxiliary terms in its Mukai vector make the cap-action kernel rigid enough to determine the unknown transcendental component. Equality in the Ext bound then permits deformation through a full polarized K3 family. A rational comparison of polarization lines and specialization reach every projective K3 surface.

===== 1.3.1 A spin-invariant tensor at the mirror quartic <ks:construction>

Retain the spaces $P_B$ and $U$ from the cohomological dictionary. Choose a rational ample class $h in "NS" \( B \)_(bb(Q))$ and set

$ N = h^perp ∩ "NS" \( B \)_(bb(Q)) \, #h(2em) V = N perp U . $

Then $dim N = 18$, while $V$ has dimension $21$ and signature $\( 2 \, 19 \)$. The square class of $q \( h \, h \)$ can be prescribed as $2 d$ for any positive integer $d$; we explain this choice when comparing polarization degrees in Section 3.4.

For this $V$, write

$ W = C^(+) \( V \) . $

The left spin action on $W$ admits a nondegenerate invariant alternating form that polarizes the Kuga–Satake structure on the chosen period component. One may use

$ E \( x \, y \) = "Tr" \( alpha x^(*) y \) \, quad "(1.21)" $ <ks:trace-form>

where $*$ reverses Clifford products and $alpha^(*) = - alpha$. For example, take $alpha$, up to sign, to be the product of two orthogonal rational vectors spanning a positive plane. At that plane, let $I$ be their normalized product. Evaluating $"Tr" \( I x^(*) I x \)$ on the even Clifford monomial basis gives a definite form: commuting the two positive generators past a monomial compensates the parity of its negative generators. Choose the sign giving positivity. Spin invariance then gives positivity on the entire component.

Let $theta in and.big^2 W$ be the nondegenerate alternating tensor corresponding to this form, and choose a rational volume element $z$ in the full Clifford algebra of $V$. Define

$ J : V arrow.r and.big^2 W \, #h(2em) J \( v \) = \( L_(v z) ⊗ 1 \) theta . quad "(1.22)" $ <ks:J>

Here $L_a$ denotes left multiplication by $a$. Since $dim V = 21$, the volume element is central and $v z$ is reversal-even. Consequently $L_(v z)$ is self-adjoint for (1.21), so (1.22) is alternating. It is also Spin-equivariant.

Restriction to the split group $"Spin" \( U \) = upright("SL")_2$ gives

$ W = M_0 ⊗ H \, #h(2em) H = bb(Q) e_(+) xor bb(Q) e_(-) . $

Thus this is precisely a torus space in the preceding dictionary, with $g = dim M_0 = 2^19$. To describe the operators on this large multiplicity space, work over $bb(C)$. The even Clifford algebra in dimension $21$ is the endomorphism algebra of its spin module $Sigma$, of dimension $2^10$. Its regular representation is therefore a sum of $2^10$ copies of $Sigma$. On restriction to $N perp U$, the module $Sigma$ is $M_1 ⊗ H$, where $M_1$ is the $2^9$-dimensional irreducible module for the full Clifford algebra of $N_(bb(C))$. Consequently

$ W_(bb(C)) = Sigma ⊗ R_0 = \( M_1 ⊗ H \) ⊗ R_0 \, #h(2em) M_(0 \, bb(C)) = M_1 ⊗ R_0 \, quad "(1.23)" $ <ks:multiplicity>

where $R_0$ is the regular-representation multiplicity space. The module $Sigma$ has an invariant alternating form. The tensor $theta$ is the product of a symmetric nondegenerate tensor $Q$ on $M_0$ with the alternating tensor on $H$. Moreover, $Q$ factors as the form on $M_1$ times a symmetric form on $R_0$.

For $y in N$, the operator $L_(y z)$ is, up to a common nonzero scale, the Clifford generator $Gamma_y$ on $M_1$ tensored with the identity on $R_0 ⊗ H$. These generators are self-adjoint for $Q$. Let $Delta$ be their chirality operator; it anticommutes with every $Gamma_y$ and is skew-adjoint, since reversal in dimension $18$ has negative sign. For $u in U$, $L_(u z)$ is, up to a common scale, $Delta$ times the vector action on $H$. It follows that $J \( y \)$ has symmetric matrix proportional to $Gamma_y Q$ and alternating $H$ factor, whereas $J \( u \)$ has matrix proportional to $Delta Q$ and belongs to the $"Sym"^2 H$ part.

The Clifford generators on $M_1$ generate its full endomorphism algebra. An operator commuting with all of them can still act arbitrarily on $R_0$. We remove this multiplicity freedom by choosing two further rational Spin-invariant nondegenerate alternating tensors $theta_1 \, theta_2$ whose relative operators to $theta$ have scalar common commutant on $R_0$. Such choices exist: over $bb(C)$ two general symmetric operators relative to the multiplicity form have this property, which is a nonempty Zariski-open condition in the rational space of invariant tensors.

We next place these tensors in separate exterior degrees. This will let an annihilation equation distinguish the wedge, derivation, and contraction parts of a torus operator. Choose a rational basis $e_1 \, e_2 \, e_3$ of $N^perp ∩ P_B$, each vector lying in one of $bb(Q) h$, $H^0 \( B \, bb(Q) \)$, and $H^4 \( B \, bb(Q) \)$. For dual bases $\( v_j \)$ and $\( v_j^or \)$ of $V$, put

$ R_1 & = 1 \, #h(2em) R_2 = theta^4 + theta_1^7 + theta_2^10 \, #h(2em) R_3 = theta^13 \,\
C_(*) & = sum_j v_j ⊗ J \( v_j^or \) + sum_(s = 1)^3 e_s ⊗ R_s . quad "(1.24)" $ <ks:test-tensor>

The first summand contains the desired Clifford map $J$. The three $e_s$ components impose the additional equations just described; after those equations remove the unwanted operators, the remaining kernel will consist of the $19$ simultaneous spin lowering directions. We prove this next. The full tensor is invariant under the simultaneous $upright("SL")_2$ action. Its inverse image under the dictionary is therefore a rational middle-degree class $a$ killed by the symplectic form.

===== 1.3.2 The formal kernel has dimension nineteen <ks:formal-kernel-section>

For a tensor in $U ⊗ and.big W$, write its three weight components as

$ t_(+) ⊗ U_(-) + t_0 ⊗ U_0 + t_(-) ⊗ U_(+) . quad "(1.25)" $ <ks:weight-components>

Here $U_(-) \, U_0 \, U_(+) in and.big W$. A superscript $*$ denotes the components of $C_(*)$; we will later use the same notation for the component in $U_B ⊗ and.big W$. In particular, $U_(-)^(*)$ is nonzero and has exterior degree two. The annihilator of $a$ by degree-two classes is computed by the operators $D_x + F \( lambda \, L \, pi \)$ in (1.15) and (1.16).

#thm([Lemma 1.8.])[
  #emph[For the class $a$ defined by (1.24), its annihilator $cal(Z) subset H^2 \( Y times T_g \, bb(Q) \)$ has dimension $19$ and projects injectively to $H^2 \( Y \, bb(Q) \)$. In particular, it satisfies (1.4).]
] <ks:formal-kernel>

#proof[
  Decompose $x = x_N + x_e + x_0 t_0$, where $x_e = sum_s x_(e \, s) e_s$. The $e_s$ components of the annihilation equation are
  $ F \( R_s \) + x_(e \, s) U_(-)^(*) = 0 . quad "(1.26)" $ <ks:e-equations>
  For $s = 2$, the second term has exterior degree two. On a power $theta_i^k$, the contraction, derivation, and wedge terms of $F$ have degrees $2 k - 2 \, 2 k \, 2 k + 2$, respectively. For $k = 4 \, 7 \, 10$ these nine degrees are distinct and exceed two. Hence $x_(e \, 2) = 0$, and each of the nine terms vanishes separately.
  Symplectic Lefschetz injectivity now gives $lambda = 0$ from $lambda and theta^4 = 0$. The derivation terms give $F_L \( theta_i \) = 0$ for $i = 0 \, 1 \, 2$, where $theta_0 = theta$. For the contraction term, contracting two $a$ directions into $theta^4$ gives, up to a nonzero factor, the two-$b$ tensor obtained from $pi$ by $Q$, wedged with $theta^2$. The same injectivity gives $pi = 0$. All these wedge maps are in the injective range for $g = 2^19$. The remaining equations (1.26) give $x_e = 0$.
  The equation $F_L \( theta \) = 0$ says that $L$ is self-adjoint for $Q$. Preservation of $theta_1$ and $theta_2$ says in addition that $L$ commutes with their relative operators. If $x = 0$, the $N$ equations become $F_L \( J \( y \) \) = 0$ for every $y in N$. By the matrix description above, these are exactly $\[ L \, Gamma_y \] = 0$. Irreducibility on $M_1$, followed by the common-commutant condition on $R_0$, makes $L$ scalar. The equation $F_L \( U_0^(*) \) = 0$ kills this scalar, because the lowering action on the highest-weight-two representation is nonzero. The kernel therefore projects injectively to $N xor bb(Q) t_0$, a space of dimension $19$.
  Conversely, each $x in N xor bb(Q) t_0$ occurs in the kernel. The operator $D_x$ is the lowering-degree element of $frak(s o) \( V \)$ displayed in (1.15). Its simultaneous spin action preserves (1.24). Since $W$ has only Cartan weights $1$ and $- 1$, its lowering action is of the form $F_L$. This proves the dimension assertion. Finally, projection of $H^3 \( Y times T_g \, bb(Q) \)$ to $H^2 \( Y \, bb(Q) \) ⊗ H^1 \( T_g \, bb(Q) \)$ identifies the map in (1.4) with the tensor product of the injective projection of $cal(Z)$ and the identity. ◻
]

===== 1.3.3 Determining the transcendental component <ks:exactness-section>

Proposition 2 now supplies a perfect complex on $B times E^g$. Divide its Mukai vector by the common nonzero rational scalar in the Euler comparison and call the result $C$. The dictionary determines $C \|_(P_B) = C_(*) \|_(P_B)$, but does not initially determine its component on $U_B = T \( B \)$. The Ext bound does. In the following statement, give the formal space $U$ the Hodge structure induced by the actual elliptic structure on $H$ through its highest-weight-two representation.

#thm([Lemma 1.9.])[
  #emph[There is a rational Hodge isometry $U_B tilde.eq U$, for the actual Hodge structure on $H = H^1 \( E \, bb(Q) \)$, under which $C = C_(*)$. The semiregularity trace of the perfect complex is injective.]
] <ks:exactness>

#proof[
  #emph[Excluding a zero transcendental component.] Choose normalized actual Hodge directions $t_(+) \, t_0 \, t_(-)$ on $U_B$, as in the cap calculation of Section 2, and write its unknown component in the form (1.25). By Lemma 9 and the Ext bound, the actual cap kernel has dimension at least $19$. Imposing the three linear conditions $x_e = 0$ leaves a subspace of dimension at least $16$. On this subspace the equations $F \( R_s \) = 0$ again imply $lambda = pi = 0$, and make $L$ self-adjoint and commuting with the two relative operators. The $N$ equations are
  $ F_L \( J \( y \) \) + q \( x_N \, y \) U_(-) = 0 #h(2em) \( y in N \) . quad "(1.27)" $ <ks:N-equation>
  The $t_(-)$ component, using (1.15), is
  $ F_L \( U_(+) \) = J \( x_N \) + q \( x_0 t_0 \, t_0 \) U_0 . quad "(1.28)" $ <ks:t-minus-equation>
  If the entire component (1.25) were zero, Equation (1.27) would make $L$ scalar, and (1.28) would give $J \( x_N \) = 0$, hence $x_N = 0$. Only the scalar $L$ and $x_0$ would remain, contrary to the dimension lower bound $16$. Thus the component is nonzero.
  #emph[Identifying the Hodge representation.] This nonzero component realizes the simple, three-dimensional K3 Hodge structure $U_B$ in the exterior algebra of copies of $H$, with the indicated Tate twists. Since $E$ is non-CM, its irreducible tensor representations are the irreducible $upright("SL")_2$ representations. The only possible one here has highest weight two. The resulting rational Hodge identification $U_B tilde.eq U$ is a similarity: the invariant forms on this irreducible representation are proportional. The underlying quadratic spaces are already rationally isometric by the dictionary. In odd dimension their similarity multiplier is a rational square, as follows by taking determinants; the signatures give the positive sign. Rescaling gives a Hodge isometry. We may now use it to compare the two sets of weight directions. The component (1.25) is equivariant for the simultaneous $upright("SL")_2$; in particular $U_(-) eq.not 0$.
  #emph[Determining the component up to one scalar.] Still on the subspace $x_e = 0$, the kernel of projection to $x_N$ has dimension at most two: setting $x_N = 0$ in (1.27) again makes $L$ scalar, leaving at most that scalar and $x_0$. The image in $N_(bb(C))$ therefore has dimension at least $14$. A totally isotropic subspace of the $18$-dimensional nondegenerate space $N_(bb(C))$ has dimension at most nine. Choose a kernel element for which $q \( x_N \, x_N \) eq.not 0$.
  For every $y perp x_N$, Equation (1.27) says that $L$ commutes with $Gamma_y$. On the irreducible Clifford module $M_1$, the commutant of these $17$ directions is spanned by $1$ and $Delta Gamma_(x_N)$; this is the restriction of the even-dimensional Clifford module to an odd-dimensional perpendicular. The relative operators again remove the multiplicity commutant, so
  $ L = l thin 1 + m thin Delta Gamma_(x_N) . $
  The Clifford relation gives
  $ \[ Delta Gamma_(x_N) \, Gamma_y \] = kappa thin q \( x_N \, y \) Delta $
  for a fixed nonzero normalization constant $kappa$. Equation (1.27) therefore makes $U_(-)$ a nonzero multiple $c U_(-)^(*)$: the two-$b$ tensor with matrix $Delta Q$ is exactly the lowest-weight line of $J \( U \)$. Equivariance gives this same scalar on all three components in (1.25).
  #emph[Determining the exact normalization.] Let $L^(*)$ be the spin lowering lift for $x = x_N$ in the tensor $C_(*)$. Comparing its version of (1.27) with the actual equation gives
  $ L = c L^(*) + tilde(l) thin 1 . $
  Now use Equation (1.28) modulo the line of $U_0$. The scalar part $tilde(l) thin 1$ of $L$ lowers $U_(+)$ into that line. The equation for $C_(*)$, with $x = x_N$, gives $F_(L^(*)) \( U_(+)^(*) \) = J \( x_N \)$. Since $U_(+) = c U_(+)^(*)$, we obtain
  $ J \( x_N \) equiv F_L \( U_(+) \) equiv c^2 F_(L^(*)) \( U_(+)^(*) \) = c^2 J \( x_N \) med \( mod med bb(C) U_0 \) . $
  One factor $c$ comes from $L$ and the other from $U_(+)$. The tensor $J \( x_N \)$ lies outside that line, since $J \( N \)$ and $J \( U \)$ have the distinct Clifford descriptions above. Hence $c^2 = 1$. Reversing the sign of the transcendental Hodge isometry if necessary gives $C = C_(*)$.
  The circle on the resulting $V = h^perp$ has its unique lift to the spin group, and its action on $W$ is the actual circle on $H^1 \( E^g \, bb(Q) \)$. Thus this actual weight-one structure is the stipulated Kuga–Satake structure. The formal and actual cap calculations now agree, so the cap kernel has dimension exactly $19$. Lemma 6 makes the semiregularity trace injective. ◻
]

===== 1.3.4 Deformation and all polarization degrees <ks:deformation>

We have constructed an exact tensor whose complex can deform whenever its Mukai vector stays Hodge. We first use this to move away from $B$ while preserving its polarization degree. We then compare that degree with any prescribed degree by a rational isometry, and specialize the exact tensor to fibers with larger Picard rank.

======= 1.3.4.1 Deformation from the mirror quartic. <deformation-from-the-mirror-quartic.>

Consider the full family of $h$-polarized K3 surfaces, coupled with their Kuga–Satake abelian varieties. These are algebraic families after a suitable level cover. More precisely, the primitive period domain and its spin cover, together with the invariant rational alternating form, give a holomorphic equivariant map to Siegel space. Arithmetic subgroups under the isogeny from Spin to $"SO"$ have arithmetic image. Intersect with the stabilizers of the chosen lattices and pass to a neat level; the K3 period theorem and algebraicity of the arithmetic quotients and their morphisms then give a smooth algebraic base with both universal families #cite(label("DeligneK3")) #cite(label("BailyBorel")) #cite(label("BorelAlgebraicity")).

The lattice at the central point may be chosen to give exactly the abelian variety $E^g$: preserve that lattice by further level restriction and multiply the rational polarization by an integer, with the sign chosen above. On this spin level cover the tensors in (1.24) are global parallel sections: the vectors $h$, $H^0$, and $H^4$ remain fixed and algebraic, the sum on $V$ is Spin-invariant, and every $theta_i$ is Spin-invariant. Hence the full Mukai vector $C_(*)$ stays Hodge throughout the family; so do its Todd factors. Lemma 10 and Proposition 7 give algebraicity of this class on every fiber of the polarized component.

On a fiber $S times A$, take the component in $H^2 \( S \) ⊗ H^2 \( A \)$ and project the K3 factor to $V = h^perp$. The additional terms $e_s ⊗ R_s$ have abelian degrees $0$, $8$, $14$, $20$, or $26$, so these algebraic projections extract $J$. To regard $J$ as a correspondence with two $H^1$ slots, use the surface and abelian Künneth projectors, the polarization and its inverse, and the inclusion of an exterior square into two ordered slots. All are algebraic: the inverse polarization is provided by the Lefschetz standard conjecture for abelian varieties, and the slot conversion can also be realized using the addition map and pullback.

This already determines the usual standard tensor, not only a tensor with the volume element on the right. Since $z$ is central, $L_(v z) = L_v R_z$ on the even space. For any nonisotropic $w in V$, replace $R_z$ by $R_w$ using right multiplication by the invertible even element $z^(- 1) w$. Every right even multiplication is a weight-one Hodge endomorphism of the abelian variety, and is therefore algebraic. Thus the map $x mapsto v x w$ is algebraic in this full primitive Clifford construction.

======= 1.3.4.2 Comparison of polarization degrees. <comparison-of-polarization-degrees.>

Fix a prescribed degree $2 d$. The initial rational ample class $h$ on $B$ can be chosen with square $2 d$. Indeed, Meyer’s theorem gives an isotropic rational vector in the indefinite rank-$19$ space $"NS" \( B \)_(bb(Q))$, hence a rational hyperbolic plane. The quadric $q \( x \, x \) = 2 d$ consequently has rational points, and rational approximation on this quadric gives a point in the open ample cone. Scaling to a primitive integral class changes its square by a rational square. Thus the primitive integral class chosen on $B$ has square $2 d$ times a rational square. Witt’s extension theorem therefore gives a rational isometry of the K3 quadratic spaces carrying its polarization line to the line of a primitive vector of square $2 d$. The isometry identifies the primitive period domains. At very general periods on both sides the surfaces have Picard rank one: the excluded wall hyperplanes, including their translates under the rational isometry, are rational hyperplanes and can all be avoided. The K3 period theorem supplies both polarized surfaces. Buskin’s theorem makes their rational Hodge isometry algebraic #cite(label("Buskin")). If $f$ denotes its restriction to the primitive spaces, the induced Clifford Hodge isomorphism satisfies

$ C^(+) \( f \) thin j_w \( v \) thin C^(+) \( f \)^(- 1) = j_(f \( w \)) \( f \( v \) \) . $

This weight-one isomorphism is induced by an abelian isogeny up to a rational multiple. Composing it with the K3 correspondence, and converting polarizations as above, therefore transports the exact tensor. We obtain the exact standard tensor very generally in degree $2 d$. One may make this comparison on marked components or on commensurable level quotients of the two domains.

======= 1.3.4.3 Specialization and transcendental restriction. <specialization-and-transcendental-restriction.>

Specialization gives the full primitive tensor at every polarized fiber. Here the class being specialized is the prescribed parallel tensor in the full Kuga–Satake family, so Picard jumps cause no loss of information. Equivalently, apply the specialization theorem and its primitive-map corollary in #cite(label("CKS")) Theorem 1.2 and Corollary 5.5: the very-general nonzero algebraic input required there has just been supplied in its stronger exact form. This specialization is the proper-cycle-parameter argument of Proposition 7; it does not replace actual algebraicity by the Hodge condition.

#proof[
  Every projective K3 surface has a primitive ample class, so the preceding construction gives its full primitive Kuga–Satake tensor. The projection to $T \( S \)$ is algebraic, using the divisor intersection pairing and the degree-zero and top-degree projectors. For the prescribed $w in T \( S \)$, restrict the even Clifford space to
  $ C^(+) \( T \( S \) \) subset C^(+) \( h^perp \) . $
  This is a rational weight-one sub-Hodge structure, and $x mapsto v x w$ preserves it for $v in T \( S \)$. It can therefore be split off by algebraic abelian correspondences. Changing its lattice, isogenous realization, or rational polarization also uses algebraic abelian correspondences. The resulting cycle has the exact action (1.20) with the chosen polarization conversion, proving the theorem. ◻
]

#thm([Corollary 1.10.])[
  #emph[For every projective complex K3 surface $S$, the rational Hodge conjecture holds in every degree on every self-power $S^m$.]
] <cor:self-powers>

#proof[
  Theorem 8 supplies the exact standard tensor on the full even Clifford space, for one nonisotropic $w$ and one polarization. These are the hypotheses of the general self-power criterion #cite(label("AKS")) Theorem 1.1. That criterion is independent of the special quadratic locus used elsewhere in the same reference to construct correspondences. ◻
]

==== 1.4 Degree-four tensors in spin families <sec:auxiliary>

The exact Kuga–Satake correspondences control each K3 surface separately. To compare several factors, we will also need a quadratic contraction tensor on certain abelian varieties. The construction below permits independent period planes at every real place of a totally real field. Its four weight-one slots will later compare actions on different spin constituents.

Let $F' \/ bb(Q)$ be a totally real Galois extension and let $\( V' \, q' \)$ be a quadratic space over $F'$ of dimension $d'$. Assume that

$ d' gt.eq 5 \, quad d' equiv 5 med \( mod med 8 \) \, #h(2em) "sig" \( V'_sigma \) = \( 2 \, d' - 2 \) quad \( sigma : F' arrow.r.hook bb(R) \) \, #h(2em) "Witt" \( V' \) gt.eq 2 . quad "(1.29)" $ <aux:quadratic-assumptions>

The dimension can be increased as needed in the application. Choose an oriented positive period plane at each real place. The corresponding spin circles give the weight-one Hodge structure

$ W' = "Res"_(F' \/ bb(Q)) C_(F')^(+) \( V' \) . quad "(1.30)" $ <aux:weight-one>

It is polarizable: choose an invariant alternating form with the required positivity at every real embedding, using approximation over $F'$ in the construction (1.21), and take its trace to $bb(Q)$. Thus $W'$ is the rational first cohomology of an abelian variety $A'$, with a chosen lattice. On each component the period construction gives an algebraic family of abelian varieties. Indeed, a neat arithmetic subgroup of the restriction-of-scalars spin group can be chosen to preserve the lattice and polarization. The product of the orthogonal period domains then maps holomorphically and equivariantly to Siegel space, and the algebraicity theorems used in Section 3.4 apply.

Choose a volume element $z'$ over $F'$. Define a rational tensor

$ cal(P) in \( "End" W' \)^(⊗ 2) quad "(1.31)" $ <aux:P>

by prescribing its extension to $bb(C)$. For each embedding $sigma : F' arrow.r.hook bb(C)$, take dual bases $\( v_j \)$ and $\( v_j^or \)$ of $V'_sigma$ and form

$ sum_j L_(v_j z') ⊗ L_(v_j^or z') . $

Each operator is supported on the $sigma$ summand of $W'_(bb(C))$; sum these tensors over $sigma$. This is the trace construction, so it descends to $bb(Q)$. It is invariant under $"Res"_(F' \/ bb(Q)) "Spin" \( V' \)$. Using an invariant polarization in each endomorphism factor, regard $cal(P)$ as a degree-four Hodge tensor in four ordered $H^1 \( A' \)$ slots.

#thm([Proposition 1.11.])[
  #emph[For the data (1.29), the tensor $cal(P)$ is the class of a cycle in $"CH"^2 \( \( A' \)^4 \)_(bb(Q))$, for every choice of the period planes and every isogenous realization of (1.30).]
] <prop:auxiliary-spin>

#proof[
  We first choose a point of the family whose abelian variety is a power of the non-CM elliptic curve $E$ in the torus dictionary. The Witt-index assumption gives a split rank-three quadratic subspace of $V'$ with signature $\( 2 \, 1 \)$ at every real place: take one hyperbolic plane and a vector positive at all places in a second one. Its spin group is $upright("SL")_2$ over $F'$. Restricting scalars and then taking the diagonal $upright("SL")_2$ over $bb(Q)$ makes $W'$ a multiple of its standard representation.
  The common circle can be placed in any prescribed component of the period family. Under the ternary identification, conjugate by an element of $upright("GL")_2 \( F' \)$ with the chosen determinant signs at the real embeddings. Approximation permits these signs to be chosen independently. This also accommodates either elliptic marking convention in the graph dictionary; its common line sign was fixed before the choice of tensor. Choose the lattice to give the corresponding elliptic power exactly at this point.
  Apply the torus-only realization with
  $ W = \( W' \)^(xor 4) = M_0 ⊗ H \, $
  using bases compatible with the four copies. Place $cal(P)$ in $and.big^4 W$, with one vector in each copy, after the polarization conversion. These copies have a second use: to verify the wedge-injectivity hypothesis, we will project a degree-one class from one copy against an annihilator’s degree-two component on another. We arrange that the annihilator has the same component on every copy.
  Let $theta$ be the direct sum of four identical copies of a nondegenerate rational Spin-invariant alternating tensor on $W'$. Choose two more tensors $theta_1 \, theta_2$ by tensoring that tensor with nondegenerate symmetric matrices on the four copy indices: the first is diagonal with distinct entries, and the second has every entry nonzero. Here the relative operators act on the four copy indices. Their commutation conditions will force identical blocks there, while allowing operators within $W'$; this is the copy condition just required. As in the earlier construction, separate the powers so that wedge, derivation, and contraction terms have different degrees. Put
  $ C_(*) = cal(P) + theta^6 + theta_1^9 + theta_2^12 . quad "(1.32)" $ <aux:test-tensor>
  This is Spin-invariant and hence invariant under the chosen simultaneous $upright("SL")_2$. The torus dictionary produces a middle-degree primitive class $a$. Here
  $ g = 1 / 2 dim_(bb(Q)) W = \[ F' : bb(Q) \] 2^(d') gt.eq 32 \, $
  so $g$ is even, the realization dimension condition is satisfied, and the symplectic wedge maps used below are in their injective range.
  We verify the wedge-injectivity hypothesis of Proposition 2. In the formal annihilation equation $F \( lambda \, L \, pi \) C_(*) = 0$, the three operator types on the displayed powers have degrees $\( 10 \, 12 \, 14 \)$, $\( 16 \, 18 \, 20 \)$, and $\( 22 \, 24 \, 26 \)$. They are all distinct and exceed the degrees $2 \, 4 \, 6$ obtained by acting on $cal(P)$. The same symplectic wedge and contraction calculation as in Lemma 9 therefore gives $lambda = pi = 0$ and
  $ F_L \( theta \) = F_L \( theta_1 \) = F_L \( theta_2 \) = 0 . $
  The first condition makes $L$ self-adjoint. Commutation with the diagonal relative operator makes it block diagonal on the four copies. Commutation with the second relative operator, whose off-diagonal entries are nonzero, then makes all four blocks equal.
  Under the inverse dictionary, the annihilator $cal(Z)$ consequently consists of degree-two forms obtained by repeating the same form on each of the four torus copies. Its projection to the degree-two cohomology of any one copy is injective. For an input in $H^1$ of copy $i$, choose $j eq.not i$ and project its wedge product to multidegree one on copy $i$ and degree two on copy $j$. This is the tensor product of the identity on $H^1$ of copy $i$ with that injective projection of $cal(Z)$. Inputs from the other copies have different multidegrees. Taking these projections for all $i$ proves (1.4).
  In the torus-only case, the Euler tests determine the entire normalized Mukai vector, so the resulting perfect complex has $C = C_(*)$. At this elliptic point, the actual cap kernel and the formal annihilator have the same dimension: the two sets of weights on this invariant tensor are conjugate over $bb(C)$. The Ext bound is therefore an equality at the cap rank, and the semiregularity trace is injective.
  Throughout the indicated algebraic period family, all terms of (1.32) remain parallel Hodge classes by Spin invariance. Apply Proposition 7 and extract cohomological degree four. The three higher powers have degrees $12$, $18$, and $24$, so the extracted class is exactly $cal(P)$. Its one-slot-per-copy component gives an algebraic class on $\( A' \)^4$. The elliptic starting point was available in every component. Finally, transport under abelian isogenies and change of rational polarization use algebraic correspondences, proving the assertion for all the stipulated realizations. ◻
]

==== 1.5 Hodge classes on mixed products <sec:mixed>

Fix projective K3 surfaces $S_1 \, dots.h \, S_m$, and put

$ V_i = T \( S_i \) \( 1 \) \, #h(2em) W_i = C^(+) \( T \( S_i \) \) . $

Here $V_i$ has weight zero, whereas $W_i$ carries the weight-one Kuga–Satake structure. Theorem 8 supplies the exact Clifford correspondences, and Corollary 11 supplies all Hodge classes on each individual self-power. We must still treat tensors involving several different $V_i$.

The individual assertions determine the projections of the joint Hodge group, but not the relations between those projections. We first use weight-one Hodge morphisms between the Kuga–Satake varieties to compare the actions of shared simple factors projectively. The CM theorem then controls the torus action. After removing these actions, only certain central signs remain, arising from the two half-spin representations in dimension four. We encode their compatibility by a finite graph and use the degree-four tensors of Proposition 12 to prove the required relations. All auxiliary abelian varieties will be chosen before choosing the tensor automorphism to which this comparison is applied.

===== 1.5.1 Tensor groups and the individual factors <tensor-groups-and-the-individual-factors>

For a finite collection of the surfaces and abelian varieties used here, let $G$ be its Hodge group, with Tate objects fixed. Let $C$ be the group of tensor automorphisms of its cohomology realizations that preserve algebraic correspondences, again fixing Tate objects. Then $G subset C$. We use these groups over $bb(Q)$ and extend their coefficients to $bb(C)$ when decomposing representations.

We recall why $C$ has the required invariant interpretation. The Lefschetz standard conjecture holds for surfaces and abelian varieties and is stable under products; it gives algebraic Künneth and Lefschetz operators in the category under consideration #cite(label("KleimanStandard")). Hodge–Riemann then identifies numerical and homological equivalence there. Numerical semisimplicity #cite(label("JannsenMotives")), with the symmetry modified to give the ordinary flip on cohomology, gives a neutral Tannakian category of pure motives with its cohomology fiber functor #cite(label("DeligneMilneTannakian")). Its tensor automorphism group is the group just described.

In weight zero, the $C$-invariants are precisely algebraic tensors. Indeed, before fixing Tate objects, invariants are morphisms from the unit motive. The central weight torus acts by cohomological weight and maps onto the group acting on Tate objects up to isogeny. It acts trivially in weight zero, so fixing Tate objects does not change those invariants. Consequently it will suffice to prove

$ C \|_(xor.big_i V_i) = G \|_(xor.big_i V_i) quad "(1.33)" $ <eq:mixed-group-goal>

for one finite collection containing the original surfaces and the auxiliary abelian varieties constructed below.

#thm([Lemma 1.12.])[
  (Individual actions).  #emph[For any such collection and any $c in C \( bb(C) \)$, the action of $c$ on $V_i$ belongs to $"Hg" \( V_i \) \( bb(C) \)$. Its simultaneous action on $V_i \, W_i$ is induced by a lift to the spin group of the quadratic space $V_i$.]
] <lem:mixed-individual>

#proof[
  Corollary 11, equivalently the exact-tensor criterion of #cite(label("AKS")) Theorem 1.1 applied after Theorem 8, makes every Hodge tensor on powers of $V_i$ algebraic. Algebraic degree projectors and the quadratic pairing allow the usual tensor criterion for the Hodge group. Hence $c \|_(V_i)$ lies in $"Hg" \( V_i \)$.
  Choose a spin lift of this orthogonal action. The operator it induces on $W_i$ preserves the exact KS tensor, all right even Clifford multiplications, and an invariant polarization. These tensors are also preserved by $c$: the KS tensor is algebraic, while the right multiplications are weight-one Hodge endomorphisms of an abelian variety and therefore algebraic. After removing the chosen spin lift, the remaining operator on $W_i$ commutes with all right multiplications, so it is left multiplication by an even Clifford element $a$. Preservation of the KS map
  $ v mapsto L_v R_w \, #h(2em) w^2 eq.not 0 \, $
  then says that $a$ commutes with every $v in V_i$. The even part of the center of the full Clifford algebra consists of scalars. Thus the remaining operator is scalar, and preservation of the polarization makes it $1$ or $- 1$. Both scalars are supplied by the spin cover. ◻
]

Let

$ G_0 = "Hg" (xor.big_i V_i xor xor.big_i W_i) . $

The projection from $G_0$ to the Hodge group of the $V_i$ has finite kernel: the Hodge circle acts on each $W_i$ through the spin lift of its action on $V_i$. Thus the $W_i$ do not add a Lie algebra factor.

For each $i$, write $E_i = "End"_(upright("Hdg")) \( V_i \)$. Zarhin’s theorem says that $E_i$ is either totally real or CM #cite(label("Zarhin")). We use the following notation:

- in the orthogonal case, $F_i = E_i$ and $d_i = dim_(E_i) V_i gt.eq 3$;
- in the unitary case, $F_i = E_i^(+)$ is the maximal totally real subfield and $d_i = dim_(E_i) V_i gt.eq 1$.

Over $bb(C)$, the corresponding groups and representations are

$ & "Hg" \( V_i \)_(bb(C)) & \( V_i \)_(bb(C))\
upright("orthogonal") & product_(p : F_i arrow.r.hook bb(C)) upright("SO")_(d_i) & upright("one standard space at each ") p\
upright("unitary") & product_(p : F_i arrow.r.hook bb(C)) upright("GL")_(d_i) & upright("a standard space and its dual at each ") p . quad "(1.34)" $ <eq:mixed-single-groups>

The quadratic form in the orthogonal case, and the underlying quadratic form of the Hermitian form in the unitary case, has positive index two at the distinguished real embedding and is negative definite at the other real embeddings. We call the distinguished embedding the #emph[active place]. The infinitesimal Hodge circle has weights $2 \, - 2$ once each on $V_i$, and weight zero elsewhere.

===== 1.5.2 Shared simple ideals and spin constituents <shared-simple-ideals-and-spin-constituents>

The derived Lie algebra of $G_0$ is a subdirect product of the simple ideals occurring in (1.34). It is therefore a product of simple ideals, each mapping diagonally onto some of the factor ideals. We call two factor ideals #emph[shared] if they are images of the same ideal of $"Lie" \( G_0 \)^(upright("der"))$.

#thm([Lemma 1.13.])[
  (Possible identifications).  #emph[Shared simple ideals have one of the following forms:]
  + #emph[orthogonal factors of the same dimension, with their standard modules matched;]
  + #emph[unitary factors of the same dimension, with their standard modules matched up to duality;]
  + #emph[ideals isomorphic to $frak(s l)_2$, shared among orthogonal factors of dimensions three or four and unitary factors of dimension two.]
  #emph[Whenever sharing occurs, the fields $F_i$, viewed in $bb(R)$ through their active embeddings, agree. The associated place identifications are then the identity on that common field. In the unitary case with $d_i gt.eq 3$, matching the standard modules as representations of the shared simple ideal also matches their characters on the connected center of $G_0$.]
] <lem:mixed-shared>

#proof[
  An identification of shared ideals must identify their infinitesimal Hodge-circle elements. Their eigenvalues first restrict the possible representations; the Galois translates of the nonzero components will then identify their fields and places. First transport a shared ideal by Galois to an active place. In an orthogonal standard module the circle element rotates one plane. For $frak(s o)_4 = frak(s l)_2 xor frak(s l)_2$, both simple ideals are active, with weights $1 \, - 1$ on their standard spin modules. The spin standard module for $frak(s o)_3$ has the same weights. In an active unitary standard module there is exactly one nonzero weight, equal to $2$ or $- 2$; the simple ideal sees its traceless part.
  These elements distinguish the possible identifications of the simple Lie algebras. In the accidental isomorphism $frak(s o)_6 tilde.eq frak(s l)_4$, the orthogonal half-spin module has weights $1 \, 1 \, - 1 \, - 1$, whereas a unitary standard module has the traceless pattern of a single nonzero weight. They cannot match. In type $D_4$, a triality automorphism carrying the standard module to a half-spin module is likewise excluded: the latter has only weights $1 \, - 1$, unlike the orthogonal standard module. The remaining coincidences are precisely those in the statement.
  For completeness, the same argument identifies the fields and the place labels. Work with simple ideals over $overline(bb(Q))$, testing activity after the fixed embedding into $bb(C)$. For an ideal at a place $p$ of $F_i$, the Galois elements carrying it to an active ideal are exactly those carrying $p$ to the active place of $F_i$. This holds also in dimension four, since both ideals there are active. The sets agree for shared ideals. After transporting one shared ideal to the active place, equality of these sets is equality of the stabilizers of the active field embeddings. Thus the embedded fields agree, and all place matchings are the matching embeddings of that field. Sharing is transported to every such place; for an orthogonal dimension-four block this may initially involve only one of its two simple ideals.
  Finally consider a shared unitary ideal of dimension $d gt.eq 3$ and choose the standard or its dual so that the simple modules agree. The exceptional eigenvalue in the traceless circle element determines the sign of its scalar part. Hence the central characters of the two matched modules have the same differential on the central component of the circle. This is true after every Galois transport. A Galois stable character condition that vanishes on the circle vanishes on the rational torus generated by its projection. Applying this to the difference of the two characters proves equality on the connected center. One may pass to powers of characters to descend to the central torus quotient; its character lattice is torsion free, so equality of these powers implies equality of the original characters. For $d = 2$, the traceless pattern does not distinguish the sign, which is why that case remains in the third alternative. ◻
]

We will use the constituents of $W_i$ as representations of the joint group. Restriction of the full even Clifford representation to an orthogonal sum gives all choices of spin constituents. In the orthogonal case, these are tensor products over the places of $F_i$: there is one spin choice for an odd-dimensional block and either half-spin choice for an even-dimensional block. Multiplicities will not matter. In dimension three the choice is the standard module of one $upright("SL")_2$; in dimension four the two choices are the standard modules of the two $upright("SL")_2$ factors.

In the unitary case the choices at a place are

$ and.big^r upright("Std") ⊗ \( det \)^(- 1 \/ 2) \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq r lt.eq d_i \, quad "(1.35)" $ <eq:mixed-unitary-spin>

with independent choices at the different places. The half-determinant notation denotes a character on the relevant spin cover. When $d_i = 2$, these choices are the standard module of $upright("SL")_2$, with zero connected central weight, and two opposite torus characters. These descriptions follow from the exterior-algebra model of spin and the spin decomposition of an orthogonal sum.

#thm([Lemma 1.14.])[
  (Weight-one comparison).  #emph[If a simple ideal is shared between factors $i$ and $j$, then $W_i$ and $W_j$ contain isomorphic representations of the joint Hodge group on which that ideal acts nontrivially. The required intertwiners and the projections onto individual copies of these representations are algebraic correspondences with complex coefficients.]
] <lem:mixed-constituents>

#proof[
  For a shared $frak(s l)_2$, choose its standard module at the given place and a transported shared standard module at every other place. Lemma 14 provides the common field and these transported identifications. In the other orthogonal types, choose matching spin constituents at all places. In the unitary types, choose matching constituents in (1.35); their central characters agree by Lemma 14. Thus the tensor constituents have the same joint Lie algebra representation. The Hodge group is connected, so they have the same group representation.
  Morphisms between weight-one Hodge structures of complex abelian varieties are induced by rational homomorphisms of abelian varieties. After extending coefficients, every intertwiner just used is therefore algebraic. The same applies to the weight-one idempotents selecting the constituents and individual multiplicity copies. ◻
]

===== 1.5.3 Removing projective and torus actions <removing-projective-and-torus-actions>

We prove the reduction before constructing the auxiliary varieties. For this subsection, allow any finite collection containing the original surfaces and their $W_i$ whose Hodge group $G$ projects onto $G_0$ with finite kernel. Write $C$ for its algebraic tensor group as above, and identify the Lie algebras of $G$ and $G_0$. The auxiliary varieties constructed below will satisfy this hypothesis. Thus the reduction will apply to the final collection once all of its objects have been fixed.

#thm([Lemma 1.15.])[
  (Projective compatibility).  #emph[For $c in C \( bb(C) \)$, its actions at all occurrences of a shared simple ideal agree in the corresponding adjoint group.]
] <lem:mixed-projective>

#proof[
  By Lemma 13, $c$ acts on each $W_i$ by a spin lift of its action on $V_i$. This has the tensor-product description of its block spin constituents, up to an overall sign. Lemma 15 provides matching constituents on which a specified shared ideal acts nontrivially. Their intertwiner is algebraic, so $c$ has the same action in these two realizations. Equality of invertible tensor-product operators forces equality of their individual factors up to scalars. A nontrivial representation of a simple Lie algebra is projectively faithful on its adjoint group. Hence the adjoint actions at the shared ideal agree. ◻
]

Let $W_(upright("CM"))$ be the sum, in all the weight-one realizations of the collection under consideration, of the constituents on which $"Lie" \( G \)^(upright("der"))$ acts trivially. This is a rational sub-Hodge structure. It is the realization of a CM abelian factor, and its projector is algebraic. The CM Hodge conjecture #cite(label("CM")) Theorem 1.1, applied jointly to these factors and their powers, therefore gives

$ C \|_(W_(upright("CM"))) = G \|_(W_(upright("CM"))) . quad "(1.36)" $ <eq:mixed-cm-image>

#thm([Lemma 1.16.])[
  (Reduction to central actions).  #emph[After multiplying $c in C \( bb(C) \)$ by an element of $G \( bb(C) \)$, one may assume that its compatible adjoint actions through the original factors are trivial at every simple ideal and that it acts as the identity on $W_(upright("CM"))$. Under these assumptions all actions on the $V_i$ outside the dimension-four orthogonal blocks and the dimension-two unitary blocks can be removed by centers of the shared simply connected derived factors.]
] <lem:mixed-central>

#proof[
  Lemma 16 gives a single prescribed action in the product of the adjoint groups, and (1.36) gives the prescribed action on the CM part. The derived group covers the first product, and the connected center covers the torus image on $W_(upright("CM"))$. These choices can be made independently. Removing their product proves the first assertion. Continue to write $c$ for the resulting element.
  On an odd-dimensional orthogonal block with trivial adjoint action, the standard action is already the identity. On an even-dimensional orthogonal block it is a sign. In a spin lift this sign is the ratio of the scalar actions on the two half-spin choices. For simple types other than $frak(s l)_2$, these ratios agree at shared ideals: apply the weight-one intertwiners to matching tensor words with either half-spin choice at the specified place. Interchanging the two choices in one description only inverts the ratio and does not change a sign. The center of the shared simply connected factor supplies that standard-module sign, so it can be removed.
  On a unitary block at $\( i \, p \)$, choose the standard or its dual so that the simple modules match on each shared ideal, as in Lemma 14. Write its scalar action as $r_(i \, p)$; the opposite choice has action $r_(i \, p)^(- 1)$. In (1.35), compare choices $0$ and $d_i$ at that place, using only these two choices at every other place. Both resulting words belong to $W_(upright("CM"))$, and their ratio is $r_(i \, p)^(d_i)$. Hence
  $ r_(i \, p)^(d_i) = 1 . quad "(1.37)" $ <eq:mixed-unitary-root>
  This removes the case $d_i = 1$. For $d_i gt.eq 3$, compare also the choice $1$ with the choice $0$. The ratio is $r_(i \, p)$, and the matched weight-one constituents show that these ratios agree on shared simple ideals. They are therefore supplied by the center of the shared $upright("SL")_(d_i)$ and can be removed there. Such derived central corrections do not affect $W_(upright("CM"))$. The only remaining actions on the $V_i$ are therefore signs on orthogonal dimension-four and unitary dimension-two blocks. ◻
]

===== 1.5.4 The graph and its auxiliary abelian varieties <the-graph-and-its-auxiliary-abelian-varieties>

We now formulate the compatibility needed for these residual signs and construct algebraic tensors that enforce it. The graph and all its auxiliary varieties depend only on the original Hodge representations, before any element of the final group $C$ is chosen.

Form a finite graph $Gamma$ as follows. Its ordinary vertices are the simple ideals of $"Lie" \( G_0 \)_(bb(C))^(upright("der"))$ isomorphic to $frak(s l)_2$. For every orthogonal factor with $d_i = 4$ and every place $p$ of $F_i$, put an edge $e = \( i \, p \)$ between the two ideals acting on that block. For every unitary factor with $d_i = 2$ and every place $p$, put an edge between its simple ideal and a new vertex $t_(i \, p)$, used only for this edge. We call these new vertices #emph[torus vertices]. Orthogonal dimension-three ideals are included as ordinary vertices, even when no edge meets them.

An edge sign $epsilon_e$ will denote the scalar action on its quadratic block after the preceding reduction. To remove all these signs using the shared derived group, we seek signs $s_v$ at the ordinary vertices, and sign $1$ at each torus vertex, such that

$ epsilon_e = s_u s_v #h(2em) \( e upright(" with endpoints ") u \, v \) . quad "(1.38)" $ <eq:mixed-sign-target>

Indeed, a central sign in $upright("SL")_2$ acts on its standard module by that sign. The orthogonal dimension-four standard module is the tensor product of the two endpoint modules, while the unitary dimension-two standard module has just the ordinary endpoint’s derived action. The same center acts trivially on the orthogonal dimension-three standard module.

The necessary compatibility is that the edge signs multiply to one around every cycle and along every path between torus vertices. These relations are also sufficient: path products from a base vertex give the signs $s_v$, as proved in Lemma 22. The role of the auxiliary tensors is to establish precisely these relations for every normalized tensor automorphism.

We therefore use every simple cycle of $Gamma$, including a cycle formed by two parallel edges, and every simple path with both endpoints torus vertices. There are finitely many. Fix one such path or cycle $gamma$. All its edges have the same real field $F$ and the same place $p$ under Lemma 14. For each embedding $tau : F arrow.r.hook bb(C)$, choose a Galois transport $gamma_tau$ of the path or cycle from $p$ to $tau$. It uses the same list of original factor indices, although its simple ideals have been transported. At the original place choose the identity transport, so that $gamma_p = gamma$.

Viewing each edge as an interval, define the #emph[grid] to be the Cartesian product of these graphs,

$ cal(G)_gamma = product_(tau : F arrow.r.hook bb(C)) gamma_tau . $

Its vertices are tuples of vertices, one at each place; a grid step changes one coordinate along an edge. A #emph[box] is a product $product_tau e_tau$, where $e_tau = \( I \( tau \) \, tau \)$ is one chosen edge of $gamma_tau$. Its vertices choose one endpoint of each edge. A square face of a box varies the endpoints at two distinct places and freezes all other vertices. At any torus endpoint we may select either of its two character lines; include all such choices in the finite construction.

#thm([Lemma 1.17.])[
  (Realization of a box).  #emph[For each box there is an auxiliary abelian variety with weight-one realization $W_square.stroked$ and an algebraic tensor 
$ cal(P)_square.stroked in \( "End" W_square.stroked \)^(⊗ 2) $

 as in Proposition 12. At a chosen complex embedding, $W_square.stroked$ contains the tensor representation attached to every vertex of the box: the standard two-dimensional representation at an ordinary vertex and the selected character line at a torus vertex, tensored over the places of $F$.]
  #emph[Adjoining all these finitely many $W_square.stroked$ to the original system does not change its Hodge Lie algebra. Vertex representations with the same choices, whether occurring in different boxes or in an original $W_i$, are identified by algebraic weight-one correspondences.]
] <lem:mixed-box>

#proof[
  Choose a totally real Galois closure $F' subset bb(R)$ of the embedded field $F$. Every embedding $q : F arrow.r.hook bb(C)$ then takes values in $F'$. For each such $q$, take the four-dimensional quadratic block of factor $I \( q \)$ and extend scalars to $F'$ through $q$. In the unitary case this is the underlying quadratic space of its two-dimensional Hermitian block. Let $V'$ be their orthogonal sum together with sufficiently many negative Tate directions.
  At every real embedding of $F'$, exactly one of the chosen blocks has a period plane: composition with that embedding permutes the places $q$, and their active places agree under the common-field identification. All other blocks are negative definite. Thus $V'$ has signature $\( 2 \, d' - 2 \)$ at every real embedding. Choose the number of negative directions so that $d'$ is sufficiently large and $d' equiv 5 med \( mod med 8 \)$. The Witt index over $F'$ is at least two. Indeed the nonarchimedean anisotropic dimension bound and the Hasse principle first give a hyperbolic plane; the residual form still has positive and negative directions at every real place and, in sufficiently large dimension, gives a second hyperbolic plane.
  Proposition 12 applies to
  $ W_square.stroked = "Res"_(F' \/ bb(Q)) C_(F')^(+) \( V' \) $
  and supplies its tensor $cal(P)_square.stroked$. The spin decomposition at the chosen embedding gives every endpoint choice in the four-dimensional blocks. The extra negative Tate directions supply only multiplicities, since the Hodge group acts trivially on them. This proves the assertion about vertex representations.
  Here is the rational group action underlying this construction. Write $Q_i$ for the four-dimensional quadratic space over $F$ of the edge’s factor, and put $Q_(i \, q) = Q_i ⊗_(F \, q) F'$. Extension of scalars through $q$ gives a rational group homomorphism
  $ "Res"_(F \/ bb(Q)) upright("SO") \( Q_i \) arrow.r "Res"_(F' \/ bb(Q)) upright("SO") \( Q_(i \, q) \) . $
  Compose these maps with the factor actions of $G_0$, take their orthogonal sum over $q$, and act trivially on the added Tate directions. This defines
  $ rho_square.stroked : G_0 arrow.r "Res"_(F' \/ bb(Q)) upright("SO") \( V' \) . $
  The fiber product of $G_0$ with the corresponding spin covers, using all the maps $rho_square.stroked$, is a rational group mapping onto $G_0$ with finite kernel. The original Hodge circle together with its auxiliary KS actions lies in this fiber product. Consequently the enlarged Hodge group projects onto $G_0$ with finite kernel and has the same Lie algebra. Matching vertex choices therefore have the same Lie representation in all their occurrences. Connectedness gives the same group representation, and the weight-one correspondence argument of Lemma 15 makes their identifications and multiplicity projections algebraic. ◻
]

Fix all the varieties in Lemma 18, and from now on let $G \, C$ denote the Hodge and algebraic tensor groups of this entire finite collection. We identify the Lie algebra of $G$ with that of $G_0$. In particular this collection satisfies the hypothesis of the preceding normalization argument. The preceding lemmas apply to this final collection. For a grid vertex $u$, let $R_u$ be the abstract tensor product of its endpoint representations. Identify it with a multiplicity copy in every occurrence by weight-one Hodge embeddings. Every intertwiner between such copies is algebraic by Lemma 18. Hence any $c in C \( bb(C) \)$ induces the same operator $c_u$ on $R_u$ through all these identifications. We may therefore choose multiplicity copies in a box using its Clifford tensor decomposition without changing $c_u$.

===== 1.5.5 The signs of a normalized tensor automorphism <the-signs-of-a-normalized-tensor-automorphism>

Now choose $c in C \( bb(C) \)$ and normalize it by Lemma 17, using the CM part of this fixed final collection. For an edge $e = \( i \, p \)$ of $Gamma$, let

$ epsilon_e in { 1 \, - 1 } $

be its scalar action on the associated quadratic block of $V_i$. For a unitary edge this is a sign by (1.37) with $d_i = 2$, and standard and dual have the same sign.

At each four-dimensional block, a lift to $upright("Spin")_4 = upright("SL")_2 times upright("SL")_2$ acts on the two endpoint modules. The normalized trivial adjoint action makes each semisimple $upright("SL")_2$ lift central, so its endpoint scalar is a sign. In the orthogonal case the two signs multiply to $epsilon_e$. In the unitary case one endpoint is a torus: if $a_e in { 1 \, - 1 }$ is the ordinary endpoint sign and $lambda_e$ is the selected torus character scalar, then $a_e lambda_e = epsilon_e$. Thus $lambda_e$ and its inverse, the opposite character scalar, are signs as well. These endpoint signs may at present depend on the edge. On a word in an original $W_i$, the action of $c$ is the product of the endpoint signs selected at its places. An overall sign of the spin lift can be absorbed into one block.

These are the edge signs in (1.38). We will prove their cycle and torus-endpoint relations by transporting a sign from one grid step to every parallel step, independently of the frozen vertices.

===== 1.5.6 Transporting a sign across the grid <transporting-a-sign-across-the-grid>

We first isolate a linear-algebra observation that will justify the subspaces used in the geometric tensor calculation.

#thm([Lemma 1.18.])[
  (Supports of an invariant pairing).  #emph[Let $A \, B$ be finite-dimensional complex vector spaces, let $U subset A$ and $V subset B$, and let $t in U ⊗ V$ induce an isomorphism $U^or arrow.r V$. If automorphisms $a$ of $A$ and $b$ of $B$ satisfy $\( a ⊗ b \) t = t$, then $a \( U \) = U$ and $b \( V \) = V$. If moreover $a \|_U = epsilon thin upright("id")$ for $epsilon in { 1 \, - 1 }$, then $b \|_V = epsilon thin upright("id")$.]
] <lem:mixed-support>

#proof[
  The images of the contraction maps $B^or arrow.r A$ and $A^or arrow.r B$ defined by $t$ are exactly $U$ and $V$. Thus preservation of $t$ preserves both subspaces. The last assertion follows from the perfect pairing, since $epsilon^(- 1) = epsilon$. ◻
]

Fix one of the paths or cycles used to construct a grid. For a step along its edge $e = \( i \, p \)$ at place $p$, freeze the vertices at every other place. If the local endpoint spaces are $X \, Y$ and the tensor product of the frozen spaces is $T$, the two grid representations are $X ⊗ T$ and $Y ⊗ T$. Inside the Hom space between them, consider

$ "Hom" \( X \, Y \) ⊗ upright("id")_T . quad "(1.39)" $ <eq:mixed-local-hom>

The reverse direction uses $"Hom" \( Y \, X \) ⊗ upright("id")_T$. Neither subspace is being assumed algebraic on its own.

#thm([Lemma 1.19.])[
  (Propagation across a box).  #emph[For the normalized element $c$, the action on (1.39), in either direction, is the scalar $epsilon_e$, independently of the frozen vertices at the other places of the grid.]
] <lem:mixed-propagation>

#proof[
  First choose every frozen vertex at an endpoint of the transported edge of factor $i$. The corresponding tensor words occur in the original $W_i$. Its endpoint-sign description shows that the action on (1.39) is $epsilon_e$ in both directions: the frozen signs cancel, and the ratio of the two endpoint signs is their product.
  It remains to move one frozen vertex across an edge at a different place $q$. Choose a box containing these two steps, with the other places fixed. Denote its four vertex representations by
  $ R_00 = X ⊗ Z ⊗ T_0 \, quad R_10 = Y ⊗ Z ⊗ T_0 \, quad R_01 = X ⊗ Z' ⊗ T_0 \, quad R_11 = Y ⊗ Z' ⊗ T_0 . $
  Project $cal(P)_square.stroked$ to
  $ "Hom" \( R_00 \, R_10 \) ⊗ "Hom" \( R_11 \, R_01 \) . quad "(1.40)" $ <eq:mixed-cross-pairing>
  The second $p$-direction has been reversed. This projection is algebraic, since its vertex projections are weight-one Hodge endomorphisms. Figure 1 records the two directions.
  #figure(
    [(310,110) (30,85)(0,0)$R_00$ (260,85)(0,0)$R_10$ (30,20)(0,0)$R_01$ (260,20)(0,0)$R_11$ (52,85)(1,0)186 (238,20)(-1,0)186 (30,66)(0,-1)28 (260,66)(0,-1)28 (145,99)(0,0)$p$-step: $epsilon_e$ (145,4)(0,0)reverse $p$-step: $epsilon_e$ (16,52)(0,0)\[r\]$q$ (274,52)(0,0)\[l\]$q$ (145,52)(0,0)$cal(P)_square.stroked$ pairs the local Hom subspaces],
    caption: [Figure 1: A square face of a box in the grid of transported paths or cycles. Only the distinct place coordinates $p$ and $q$ vary. The projected tensor pairs the local Hom subspace represented by the upper $p$-arrow with the one represented by the reversed lower arrow. Preservation of this perfect pairing transports the scalar sign when the frozen $q$-vertex changes. The vertical arrows indicate that change of vertex, not a chosen linear map. ],
  ) <fig:mixed-grid>
  We verify that the tensor in (1.40) is a perfect pairing between the two local Hom subspaces. Only the $p$-block of $V'$ contributes. Clifford multiplication by a vector in that four-dimensional block interchanges its two half-spin spaces and acts as the identity on spectator slots, up to fixed grading signs. In a graded Clifford tensor model these signs are the chirality operators on preceding even-dimensional slots; after choosing the spectators they are scalars. The volume element in the total odd-dimensional Clifford module likewise acts as a scalar. Choose multiplicity copies in this Clifford tensor decomposition and transport the projected tensor to the fixed tensor models $R_u$. Its spectator factors are then identities; the transported actions are the already fixed operators $c_u$.
  For the two two-dimensional half-spin spaces $X \, Y$, the vector representation is $X ⊗ Y$, and Clifford multiplication gives all of $"Hom" \( X \, Y \)$ and all of $"Hom" \( Y \, X \)$. More explicitly, with the skew-form identifications $j_X : X arrow.r X^or$ and $j_Y : Y arrow.r Y^or$, the two maps belonging to $x ⊗ y$ are, up to nonzero scalars,
  $ y ⊗ j_X \( x \) \, #h(2em) x ⊗ j_Y \( y \) . $
  The inverse quadratic metric therefore gives a nondegenerate tensor between the two Hom spaces, with identities on the frozen factors. If an endpoint is a torus character line in $Y$, project to that line. The first Hom space consists of maps from $X$ into the selected line, and the second of maps from that same line to $X$. In the displayed formulas these use respectively the selected character component of $y$ and its opposite under $j_Y$. They remain perfectly paired by the metric. Thus the projected tensor has exactly the local Hom subspaces as its minimal tensor supports.
  Since $c$ preserves $cal(P)_square.stroked$ and all the vertex projections, Lemma 19 first makes these Hom subspaces invariant and then transfers the sign $epsilon_e$ from the upper one to the lower one. Reversing the roles of the two directions gives the reverse propagation as well. Moving the frozen places successively along their paths proves the assertion for every vertex choice. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 1.20.])[
  (Cycle and endpoint relations).  #emph[The edge signs of $Gamma$ satisfy 
$ product_(e in gamma) epsilon_e = 1 quad "(1.41)" $ <eq:mixed-cycle-signs>

 for every cycle $gamma$ and for every path $gamma$ whose two endpoints are torus vertices.]
] <lem:mixed-cycle>

#proof[
  It suffices to use simple cycles and simple paths, since cancellation of repeated edges reduces the general case to them. Fix one such path or cycle and a place $p$. Freeze all other places at the starts of their transported paths. At the initial grid vertex, the action of $c$ is scalar: all these endpoint choices occur in the original representation belonging to the first edge.
  Follow the path at place $p$. Suppose $c$ is scalar $a$ on its current vertex representation. By Lemma 20, conjugation on the local Hom space for the next edge $e$ is $epsilon_e$. Hence
  $ c_(upright("next")) thin f = epsilon_e a f #h(2em) upright("for every ") f in "Hom" \( X \, Y \) ⊗ upright("id")_T . $
  The images of these maps span the next vertex representation. Thus its scalar is $epsilon_e a$. Around a cycle one returns to the same representation and obtains (1.41). For a torus-to-torus path, both endpoint words consist only of character slots, including at the frozen places. They belong to $W_(upright("CM"))$, so their scalars are both one. The same calculation proves the endpoint relation. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 1.21.])[
  (Vertex signs).  #emph[There are signs $s_v$ at the vertices of $Gamma$, with $s_v = 1$ at every torus vertex, such that 
$ epsilon_e = s_u s_v quad upright("for every edge ") e upright(" with endpoints ") u \, v . $

]
] <lem:mixed-node-signs>

#proof[
  In each connected component choose a base vertex, choosing a torus vertex when one is present, and give it sign one. Define the sign of any other vertex as the product of the edge signs along a path from the base. The cycle relations in Lemma 21 make this independent of the path. If another torus vertex occurs, the endpoint-path relation gives it sign one. The edge formula follows immediately. Isolated vertices may also be assigned sign one. ◻
]

===== 1.5.7 Completion of the mixed-product argument <completion-of-the-mixed-product-argument>

#proof[
  Take $c in C \( bb(C) \)$ for the final collection fixed after Lemma 18. Lemma 17 removes an element of $G \( bb(C) \)$ so that the only remaining actions on the $V_i$ are the edge signs of $Gamma$. By Lemma 22, these are products of signs assigned to the shared $frak(s l)_2$ ideals, with sign one at the torus vertices. Use those signs in the centers of the corresponding simply connected derived factors. On an orthogonal dimension-four block their action is the product of the two endpoint signs; on a unitary dimension-two standard block it is the ordinary-vertex sign. Their action on orthogonal dimension-three blocks is trivial. They therefore remove every remaining action on the $V_i$. This proves (1.33) over $bb(C)$, and hence over $bb(Q)$.
  Every rational Hodge tensor in the $V_i$ is now fixed by $C$ and is therefore algebraic. Apply the algebraic Künneth projectors to each factor of $S_1 times dots.h.c times S_m$. Besides the transcendental summands in degree two, the pieces are generated by the unit, divisors, and the point class, all algebraic. Thus every Hodge class in a Künneth summand is a sum of the algebraic transcendental tensors just obtained tensored with these elementary classes. Products and the projectors realize them in the required codimension. This proves surjectivity of the rational cycle-class map for every ordered list, including repetitions, and every codimension in the statement. ◻
]

== 2 The realization theorem <part:realization>

The preceding argument reduces Theorem 1 to Proposition 2. We now prove that proposition. Its two conclusions, prescribed Euler pairings and a bound on $"Ext"^2$, will come from one Lagrangian immersion: its fundamental class controls the pairings, while its second cohomology controls the extension space. The construction must also remove the disk obstruction and identify the category generated by the embedded mirror tests. These are the successive tasks of this part.

==== 2.1 A Lagrangian immersion with controlled cohomology <sec:topology>

We begin the proof of Proposition 2 by constructing a geometric representative of its input class $a$. Two features of that representative will control its Floer theory: ambient classes detect its degree-two cohomology, and its self-intersections contribute only odd-degree generators. We establish these features together. All homology and cohomology in this section have rational coefficients unless otherwise indicated.

#thm([Proposition 2.1.])[
  #emph[Let $D = Y times T_g$, $omega$, and $n$ be as in Section 1, with $g$ even and $n gt.eq 12$. Suppose that $a in H^n \( D \, bb(Q) \)$ satisfies $omega a = 0$, and that its annihilator 
$ cal(Z) = { x in H^2 \( D \, bb(Q) \) : x a = 0 } $

 satisfies the injectivity hypothesis (1.4). There exist a closed connected spin $n$-manifold $L$, a positive integer $nu$, and a Maslov-zero Lagrangian immersion $i : L arrow.r D$ such that 
$ "PD" thin i_(*) \[ L \] = nu a \, quad "(2.1)" $ <topo:class>

 
$ i^(*) : H^2 \( D \, bb(Q) \) \/ cal(Z) arrow.r^(med tilde.op med) H^2 \( L \, bb(Q) \) \, quad "(2.2)" $ <topo:h2>

 and 
$ i_(*) : H_(n - 2) \( L \, bb(Q) \) arrow.r H_(n - 2) \( D \, bb(Q) \) quad upright("is injective") . quad "(2.3)" $ <topo:push>

 The immersion is surjective on fundamental groups and has only transverse double points, with no triple points. With an integer grading whose orientation agrees with the orientation of $L$, both ordered branch indices at every double point are odd.]
] <prop:immersion>

We first realize the class with the required tangential structure and low cohomology. The immersion theorem then supplies a graded Lagrangian representative. To control the double-point signs, we place all of its crossings inside a contractible exact domain and apply the flexibility results of Eliashberg–Murphy and Ekholm–Eliashberg–Murphy–Smith. This adapts the local-domain construction of #cite(label("A8")) Section 3; the choice of filling classes below also treats the nonzero $pi_2$ of the K3 factor.

===== 2.1.1 Tangential realization and low-dimensional surgery <tangential-realization-and-low-dimensional-surgery>

We seek a representative whose degree-two cohomology is exactly $H^2 \( D \, bb(Q) \) \/ cal(Z)$ and whose complexified tangent bundle is identified with the ambient tangent bundle. We impose these two conditions by lifting the representing map: a torus bundle kills the classes in $cal(Z)$, and a spin pullback records the required stable tangent identification. The following calculation shows that the lift introduces no further degree-two classes; surgery will then transfer this degree-two description to the representing manifold.

Choose independent integral classes $z_1 \, dots.h \, z_r$ spanning $cal(Z)$ over $bb(Q)$, and let $pi_0 : cal(T)_0 arrow.r D$ be the principal $r$-torus bundle with these Chern classes. Choose the natural stable $"SU"$ structure on $T D$, and form the homotopy pullback

$ cal(T) = cal(T)_0 times_(B "SU")^h B "Spin" \, #h(2em) pi : cal(T) arrow.r D \, $

where $B "Spin" arrow.r B "SU"$ is complexification. The tautological stable real bundle $xi$ on $cal(T)$ has virtual rank $n$, is spin, and comes with a stable identification

$ xi ⊗ bb(C) tilde.eq pi^(*) T D quad "(2.4)" $ <topo:tautological>

that retains the determinant trivializations.

#thm([Lemma 2.2.])[
  #emph[Pullback induces 
$ H^2 \( cal(T) \, bb(Q) \) tilde.eq H^2 \( D \, bb(Q) \) \/ cal(Z) . $

 Moreover, $"PD"^(- 1) a in H_n \( D \, bb(Q) \)$ is in the image of $pi_(*) : H_n \( cal(T) \, bb(Q) \) arrow.r H_n \( D \, bb(Q) \)$.]
] <topo:lift>

#proof[
  The compact Kähler manifold $D$ is formal over $bb(R)$ #cite(label("DeligneGriffithsMorganSullivan")). We first compute over $bb(R)$. A real model for its torus bundle is obtained from $H^(*) \( D \, bb(R) \)$ by adjoining degree-one generators $u_1 \, dots.h \, u_r$ with $d u_j = z_j$. In degree two, the horizontal classes give $H^2 \( D \, bb(R) \) \/ \( cal(Z) ⊗ bb(R) \)$. The differential on $H^1 \( D \, bb(R) \) ⊗ chevron.l u_1 \, dots.h \, u_r chevron.r$ is the scalar extension of the map in (1.4), and is injective. The differential on $and.big^2 chevron.l u_1 \, dots.h \, u_r chevron.r$ is also injective:
  $ d \( u_j u_l \) = z_j u_l - z_l u_j \, $
  and the $z_j$ are independent. The images of these two differentials have different vertical degrees, so cannot cancel.
  The fiber of $B "Spin" arrow.r B "SU"$ is two-connected. Over $bb(R)$, complexification takes each even-index Chern generator to a nonzero multiple of a free Pontryagin generator, and every odd-index Chern generator to zero. In the relative Koszul model for the second pullback, eliminate the even-index pairs. What remains is the torus bundle model with generators of degrees $4 j + 1$, $j gt.eq 1$, whose differentials are the odd Chern classes of $T D$. These classes vanish: $T D$ is the sum of the tangent bundle of a K3 surface, when present, and a trivial bundle. In particular the second pullback changes neither the preceding degree-two calculation nor its pullback map.
  Define a functional on the degree-$n$ part of this model by pairing a horizontal class $v in H^n \( D \, bb(R) \)$ with $a$, and setting the functional equal to zero on terms with positive vertical degree. It is a cycle in the dual complex. Indeed, a differential with a horizontal term is a sum of multiples of the $z_j$, and $a z_j = 0$; the remaining new generators have zero differential. Its induced real homology class maps to $"PD"^(- 1) a$. The cohomology pullback and homology pushforward are defined over $bb(Q)$. Faithfulness of scalar extension from $bb(Q)$ to $bb(R)$ therefore descends the asserted kernel and isomorphism, as well as membership of the rational class $"PD"^(- 1) a$ in the image of pushforward. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 2.3.])[
  #emph[There are a closed connected spin $n$-manifold $L_0$, a map $f : L_0 arrow.r D$, and a positive integer $nu$ satisfying (2.1)–(2.3) with $f$ in place of $i$. They admit a stable identification 
$ T L_0 ⊗ bb(C) tilde.eq f^(*) T D quad "(2.5)" $ <topo:stable>

 with the specified determinant homotopy. The map is surjective on fundamental groups, and $L_0$ has two additional free circle generators mapping trivially to $D$. After fixing $nu$, its Euler characteristic can be made arbitrarily large while preserving these assertions.]
] <topo:source>

#proof[
  Use the spin bundle $xi$ on $cal(T)$, including (2.4), as a stable tangential structure. With our virtual-rank-$n$ convention, the group of $n$-dimensional bordism classes carrying this structure is $pi_0 "Th" \( - xi \)$ by the Pontryagin–Thom construction; see #cite(label("Thom1954")) and #cite(label("RathelFournier2024")) Section 2.3. Rational stable homotopy and the Thom isomorphism give
  $ pi_0 "Th" \( - xi \) ⊗ bb(Q) tilde.eq H_0 \( "Th" \( - xi \) \; bb(Q) \) tilde.eq H_n \( cal(T) \; bb(Q) \) . $
  Thus a positive integral multiple of the lift in Lemma 24 is represented by a closed manifold mapping to $cal(T)$, with its stable tangent bundle identified with the pullback of $xi$. Composing with $pi$ gives (2.5) and the required fundamental class. We now modify the representative by bordisms over $cal(T)$.
  Put $Gamma = pi_1 \( cal(T) \)$. The torus-bundle homotopy sequence exhibits its fundamental group as an extension of $pi_1 \( D \)$ by a quotient of $bb(Z)^r$; the second pullback has two-connected fiber. Consequently $Gamma$ is polycyclic, and $Gamma arrow.r pi_1 \( D \)$ is surjective. The space $cal(T)$ has a CW model with finite skeleta in the degrees needed here: the base and torus bundle are compact, and the characteristic-class pullback uses classifying spaces and loop spaces of finite type. The group ring $bb(Z) Gamma$ is Noetherian #cite(label("Hall1954")).
  Join the components of the representative by one-handles and add loops mapping to generators of $Gamma$. Its fundamental-group map is then surjective. Its kernel is normally finitely generated, since both groups are finitely presented. Surgery on disjoint embedded representatives of normal generators makes that map an isomorphism. Each surgery uses a null homotopy over $cal(T)$; a twist of the normal framing on a circle makes the stable tangential data extend.
  Next make the map on $pi_2$ surjective by adding $S^2 times S^(n - 2)$ summands mapped through $S^2$, for a finite set of module generators of the missing classes. Such a pulled-back spin bundle is stably trivial over $S^2$, so these additions carry the prescribed tangential data. The kernel on $pi_2$ is finitely generated as a $bb(Z) Gamma$-module: this follows from the finite cellular chain modules of the universal covers and Noetherianity. Represent its generators by disjoint embedded two-spheres and perform surgery on them, using their null homotopies over $cal(T)$. Their normal bundles are trivial: the spin condition makes their stable normal bundles trivial, and the rank is in the stable range. The stable framing extends over surgery because $pi_2 "SO" = 0$. Since $n gt.eq 12$, the embedding dimensions and the reverse-handle indices preserve the already established fundamental-group assertion. These operations make $pi_2$ an isomorphism as well. The resulting map to $cal(T)$ consequently induces an isomorphism on $H^2$.
  Now take connected sums with two copies of $S^1 times S^(n - 1)$, and with as many copies of $S^4 times S^(n - 4)$ as desired, mapping these summands constantly to $cal(T)$. These products are stably parallelizable and preserve the tangential structure and pushed fundamental class. They do not change $H^2$. The first two summands provide the extra null-image circles; each subsequent summand raises the Euler characteristic by two. Lemma 24 therefore proves (2.2).
  To verify (2.3), write any $u in H_(n - 2) \( L_0 \, bb(Q) \)$ as $f^(*) x ∩ \[ L_0 \]$ using Poincaré duality and the surjectivity in (2.2). The projection formula gives
  $ f_(*) u = x ∩ f_(*) \[ L_0 \] = nu thin "PD"^(- 1) \( x a \) . $
  If this is zero, then $x in cal(Z)$ and $f^(*) x = 0$, so $u = 0$. ◻
]

Since $omega in cal(Z)$, the map just constructed satisfies $f^(*) \[ omega \] = 0$. Destabilize (2.5) in the unitary stable range over the $n$-dimensional manifold $L_0$, retaining the determinant homotopy. Restriction to the real tangent bundle gives a formal Lagrangian immersion of Maslov class zero. The Gromov–Lees theorem realizes it by a Lagrangian immersion homotopic to $f$ #cite(label("GromovLees")) #cite(label("RathelFournier2024")). We continue to call this immersion $f$, and choose its integer grading with the orientation of $L_0$.

===== 2.1.2 Chosen fillings of zero area <chosen-fillings-of-zero-area>

The remaining task is to alter the double points without changing the cohomological properties. To do this inside a contractible exact domain, the loops formed by the two branches of each crossing must admit chosen fillings of zero symplectic area. No uniqueness of a filling area is asserted or required.

#thm([Lemma 2.4.])[
  #emph[After Lagrangian perturbations, $f$ is self-transverse without triple points, and there are pairwise disjoint embedded arcs in $L_0$ joining the two preimages of each double point such that every image loop has a chosen filling in $D$ of symplectic area zero. The perturbations preserve the homotopy class and integer grading.]
] <topo:zero-actions>

#proof[
  Choose fillings for the two extra null-image circles from Lemma 25. In an immersed cotangent neighborhood, replacing the zero section by a small closed one-form changes each chosen filling area by the corresponding period, up to a fixed sign. The two periods can be adjusted independently. Choose the resulting areas to generate a dense subgroup of $bb(R)$. Concatenations and reversals of these circles are given the concatenated and reversed fillings, so their chosen areas add. Small exact perturbations now make the immersion self-transverse and remove triple points, without changing these periods.
  For a double point $f \( a_j \) = f \( b_j \)$, begin with a path from $a_j$ to $b_j$. Surjectivity on fundamental groups allows insertion of a source loop that makes its image contractible. Choose a filling. By inserting combinations of the two null-image circles with their chosen fillings, make its area arbitrarily small. Choose disjoint source patches at the $a_j$, avoiding every other double-point preimage. An exact perturbation by $d F_j$, where $F_j$ is constant near $a_j$ and zero off its patch, changes this path’s filling area by $F_j \( b_j \) - F_j \( a_j \)$, up to the same sign convention. It fixes the sheet near the crossing. Thus it makes the area exactly zero. The endpoint formula remains valid if the path visits the patch several times. With the patches fixed, a uniform sufficiently small bound on these perturbations preserves all crossings and creates no new ones; take the preceding residual areas within that bound.
  Finally, homotopies of the source paths relative to their endpoints preserve the chosen areas, because $f^(*) omega = 0$ as a differential form. General position makes the finitely many paths embedded, mutually disjoint, and disjoint from the other double-point preimages. Grading and spin data transport through the Lagrangian perturbations. ◻
]

===== 2.1.3 A contractible exact neighborhood <a-contractible-exact-neighborhood>

#thm([Lemma 2.5.])[
  #emph[There is a compact contractible Weinstein domain $\( P \, lambda \) subset \( D \, omega \)$, with $d lambda = omega \|_P$, such that $W = f^(- 1) \( P \)$ is a disk containing all double-point preimages. Its primitive satisfies $f^(*) lambda \|_W = 0$, and its Liouville field is tangent to the immersed sheets. The immersion is cylindrical along $partial W$. At one interior point, $W$ has a single linear sheet in a radial Darboux ball.]
] <topo:exact-domain>

#proof[
  The source and target neighborhoods have different topology. A tree joining the double-point preimages has a disk as its source thickening, but its image acquires a loop at each double point. We first thicken that image to a Weinstein zero- and one-handlebody $P_1$. We then attach two-handles along its free generators, with cores disjoint from the immersion. The target becomes contractible while its preimage remains a disk. We give the primitive, collar, and filling details needed for this construction, adapting #cite(label("A8")) Lemma 3.5.
  #emph[Thickening the tree and its image.] Extend the arcs of Lemma 26 to an embedded tree $Theta subset L_0$ by connector arcs avoiding all other sheet intersections. Its image $G = f \( Theta \)$ has precisely the identifications at the double-point pairs. Each chosen source arc becomes one of the free graph cycles, with its previously chosen zero-area filling. Subdivide an ordinary edge to obtain a vertex with only one preimage. Figure 2 illustrates the resulting spines.
  #figure(
    [(420,105) (10,53)(1,0)90 (10,53) (35,53) (75,53) (100,53) (10,67)(0,0)$a_1$ (35,67)(0,0)$b_1$ (75,67)(0,0)$a_2$ (100,67)(0,0)$b_2$ (55,19)(0,0)source tree $Theta$ (55,5)(0,0)thickening: $W$ (119,53)(1,0)31 (134,68)(0,0)$f$ (177,53) (241,53) (193,53) (225,53) (193,53)(1,0)32 (209,19)(0,0)image graph $G$ (209,5)(0,0)thickening: $P_1$ (271,53)(1,0)31 (321,53) (385,53) (321,53) (385,53) (337,53) (369,53) (337,53)(1,0)32 (321,53)(0,0)$e_1^2$ (385,53)(0,0)$e_2^2$ (353,19)(0,0)filled spine (353,5)(0,0)thickening: $P$],
    caption: [Figure 2: Schematic spines for two double points. Identifying $a_j$ with $b_j$ turns a contractible source tree into a graph with loops. Two-handles kill these loops in the target; their filling interiors miss the immersion, so the preimage remains the disk $W$. The picture represents the abstract spines, not an embedding in $D$. ],
  ) <fig:topo-spines>
  At each vertex take a small radial Darboux ball. The single sheet, or the two transverse sheets at a double point, can be straightened simultaneously. For two sheets, first make one horizontal; the other is a gradient graph over the vertical plane, and a symplectic shear straightens it while fixing the first. Arrange the incident tree edges as distinct radial rays. Thin Weinstein one-handles along the remaining image arcs join these balls and carry strips in the corresponding source sheets.
  Here are compatible models for these attachments and the two-handles used below. In symplectic coordinates $\( u \, v \, z \)$, with $u \, v in bb(R)^j$ and $z in bb(R)^(2 \( n - j \))$, use the Liouville field
  $ Z_j = \( - u \, 2 v \, z \/ 2 \) \, #h(2em) j = 1 \, 2 . quad "(2.6)" $ <topo:handle-field>
  A box $\| u \| lt.eq 1$, $\| \( v \, z \) \| lt.eq epsilon$ has negative face near $\| u \| = 1$, isotropic core $v = z = 0$, and a quadratic Lyapunov function with one critical point of index $j$. For $j = 1$, the subspace $v = 0$ with $z$ in a fixed Lagrangian plane is an invariant Lagrangian strip.
  The attaching contact identifications can be chosen strict for the specified contact forms. Indeed, near an isotropic attaching locus choose a slice tangent to the contact plane along that locus. The Reeb flow writes the contact form as $d t + beta$ on a neighborhood of the slice. Match the slices by the symplectic isotropic neighborhood theorem with the prescribed tangent and symplectic normal data. The resulting difference of primitives is a closed form that restricts to zero on the attaching locus. It is $d h$, with $h = 0$ there, since the neighborhood retracts onto the locus. Adjusting the Reeb coordinate by $h$ makes the contact identification strict. The same argument applies to the Legendrian patches in the sheets. Extending by Liouville flow gives collars preserving the primitives.
  For the one-handles, choose transverse strip framings along the source arcs and extend the prescribed end germs over each strip. The relative Lagrangian neighborhood theorem extends these maps symplectically with the collar germs fixed: after fixing tangent data, the remaining linear lifts are contractible, and the tubular construction and Moser argument are relative to the collars. Shrinking the handles keeps them embedded, mutually disjoint away from their attachments, and disjoint from every other sheet. The model primitives glue through the strict collars. After rounding the positive corners, we obtain a Weinstein zero- and one-handlebody $P_1$ with spine $G$. Its preimage is a thickening of the source tree, hence a disk, and its primitive vanishes on the sheets. The singled-out radial ball can be retained inside $P_1$.
  #emph[Isotropic fillings outside the one-handlebody.] The loops of $P_1$ now have to be killed without meeting any source sheet. The fillings must retain their chosen zero-area classes: contractibility of the attaching circles alone would not control area in the presence of $pi_2 \( D \)$. Choose a free basis of $pi_1 \( P_1 \)$ represented by disjoint isotropic circles in $partial P_1$, avoiding the Legendrian boundary of the source disk. These representatives exist by the relative subcritical contact isotropic embedding theorem #cite(label("EliashbergMishachev2002")) Theorem 12.4.1; formal tangent lines can be moved into the contact distribution, and all the avoidance conditions are in the general-position range.
  Each attaching circle inherits a zero-area filling in $D$: join it in $P_1$ to its graph cycle by an annulus and append the chosen filling. Both boundary primitive integrals vanish, so the annulus has area zero. We may represent this same filling class outside $P_1$. In fact, recovering a $2 n$-dimensional zero- and one-handlebody from its boundary uses only handles of indices $2 n$ and $2 n - 1$. The inclusion of the exterior into $D$ therefore has no relative homotopy in the dimensions of this disk and its deformation. This preserves the chosen relative disk class, not just the contractibility of the attaching circle. Consequently its symplectic area remains zero even when $pi_2 \( D \) eq.not 0$. Prescribe the initial collar to be the outward Liouville cylinder. General position relative to these collars gives embedded filling disks with disjoint interiors, all missing $f \( L_0 \)$.
  We next make the disks isotropic while retaining those collars. Use a prequantization circle bundle for the integral form $omega$. The collars lift horizontally. In a trivialization over a filling disk, the zero area gives winding number zero for its boundary lift, so this lift extends over the disk. The space of isotropic ordered two-frames in the contact distribution retracts to $upright(U) \( n \) \/ upright(U) \( n - 2 \)$ and is simply connected. Thus the prescribed isotropic derivative on the collar extends as a formal isotropic monomorphism over the disk. The ordinary real two-frame space in rank $2 n + 1$ is two-connected, so the derivative of the lifted disk is homotopic to that monomorphism relative to the collar. The relative, $C^0$-dense subcritical contact embedding $h$-principle realizes these data, fixing a slightly smaller collar.
  Projection gives isotropic immersions of the disks into $D$. They can be chosen embedded and mutually disjoint: contact Hamiltonian variations near distinct lift points give arbitrary independent projected variations, and $2 + 2 < 2 n$. The same argument preserves avoidance of the immersed source because $2 + n < 2 n$. Smallness protects avoidance away from the fixed collars; the collars themselves protect the attachments.
  #emph[Attaching the two-handles.] We now have embedded isotropic disks with disjoint interiors outside $P_1$, prescribed outward collars, and no intersections with the immersion. Choose symplectic normal framings over these disks. They restrict to the contact symplectic normal framings needed at the attaching circles. Parameterize the attaching circles by parameterizations extending over the disks. The relative isotropic neighborhood theorem and relative Moser argument now embed thin index-two handles with the prescribed strict collars and model (2.6). These handles miss the immersion. Their primitives glue, and rounding corners produces $P$.
  Its spine is the graph $G$ with a two-cell attached along each free generator. After collapsing a maximal tree, these attaching maps form a free basis of a bouquet of circles; the resulting complex is contractible. Thus $P$ is contractible. The two-handles have not changed the preimage disk, its primitive-zero sheets, or its outer cylindrical collar. The radial single-sheet ball also remains intact. ◻
]

We have now localized all double points in an exact simply connected target. This is what permits their algebraic signed count, rather than a fundamental-group-valued intersection invariant, to control the final replacement.

===== 2.1.4 Double-point signs and the integer grading <double-point-signs-and-the-integer-grading>

#proof[
  Write $n = 2 k$. For the immersion supplied by the preceding lemmas, the self-intersection formula reads
  $ 2 I \( f \) = f_(*) \[ L_0 \] dot.op f_(*) \[ L_0 \] - \( - 1 \)^k chi \( L_0 \) . quad "(2.7)" $ <topo:double-number>
  Here the normal Euler number is $\( - 1 \)^k chi \( L_0 \)$, since the normal bundle of an oriented Lagrangian is its tangent bundle with this orientation factor. In Lemma 25, choose the Euler characteristic large enough that $I \( f \)$ is nonzero and has sign $\( - 1 \)^(k - 1)$. Put $d = \| I \( f \) \|$.
  Complete the domain $P$ of Lemma 27 by its positive cylindrical end, extend $W$ by its cylinder, and remove the center of its single-sheet radial ball. The punctured ball is a negative Liouville end. The source is now $S^(n - 1) times bb(R)$, with the standard real-plane Legendrian sphere $phi.alt_0$ at that end. The target is simply connected: $P$ was contractible, and removing a point in dimension $2 n$ does not change its fundamental group.
  The stabilization traces of #cite(label("EEMS")) Lemma 3.2 and proof of Lemma 3.4 have signs
  $ phi.alt arrow.r phi.alt_1 : med \( - 1 \)^(k - 1) \, #h(2em) phi.alt_1 arrow.r phi.alt : med \( - 1 \)^k \, quad "(2.8)" $ <topo:trace-signs>
  where $phi.alt_1$ is a loose stabilization and the arrows run outward along the symplectization. Insert $d$ reverse traces in separated collars near the puncture, from the $d$-fold stabilization $phi.alt_d$ at the negative end to $phi.alt_0$ outward. The new negative link is connected and loose, and its total signed double count is zero by (2.7).
  Eliashberg–Murphy’s exact cancellation theorem #cite(label("EliashbergMurphyCaps")) Theorem 2.3 now applies. The target is a simply connected Liouville manifold with a negative end; the immersion is proper, cylindrical there, self-transverse, and has finitely many double points. It is exact because $H^1 \( S^(n - 1) times bb(R) \; bb(R) \) = 0$. The loose component and vanishing signed count were just arranged. The theorem’s extra width condition concerns source dimension three, so is irrelevant here. We obtain a compactly supported Hamiltonian regular homotopy to an embedding, leaving both cylindrical tails fixed.
  In the unchanged negative tail insert $d$ forward traces from $phi.alt_0$ to $phi.alt_d$, and fill the center by the real plane. The resulting disk immersion has exactly $d$ double points, all of sign $\( - 1 \)^(k - 1)$. Its noncylindrical part is compact. A sufficiently long negative Liouville flow in the filled positive completion brings that part inside $P$. Backward flow exists for all time; points of $P$ cannot leave it backward, while the positive end is translated toward $P$. The flow rescales the symplectic form and hence preserves Lagrangianity. The cylindrical set is unchanged, so its intersection with the original outer collar is unchanged. Trim at that collar and glue to the immersion outside $W$.
  This replaces a source disk by a source disk with the same collar. A collar identification extends continuously over the disks, and their maps into the contractible $P$ are homotopic relative to the collar. It follows that the pushed fundamental class and the maps on degree-two and degree-$\( n - 2 \)$ (co)homology are unchanged, under the identifications through the common complement. The same complement carries every first homology class and the fundamental-group map. The boundary sphere has a unique spin structure, so spin extends over the replacement disk. The Maslov class remains zero. Choose the extended integer grading with the original orientation parity.
  For graded oriented Lagrangian intersections in real source dimension $2 k$, a generator of degree $\| x \|$ has intersection sign
  $ \( - 1 \)^k \( - 1 \)^(\| x \|) . $
  The sign $\( - 1 \)^(k - 1)$ obtained above therefore makes every branch degree odd. Reversing the ordering replaces its degree by $n - \| x \|$, which is also odd because $n$ is even. The disk replacement has only transverse double points and introduces no triples or intersections with the unmodified source. Together with Lemma 25, this proves all assertions. ◻
]

==== 2.2 Removing the disk obstruction <sec:curvature>

Let $i : L arrow.r D$ be the graded spin immersion of Proposition 23. Its transverse self-intersection generators have odd integer degrees, and

$ H^2 \( L \, bb(Q) \) tilde.eq H^2 \( D \, bb(Q) \) \/ cal(Z) \, #h(2em) i_(*) : H_(n - 2) \( L \, bb(Q) \) arrow.r H_(n - 2) \( D \, bb(Q) \) upright(" is injective") . quad "(2.9)" $ <curv:topological-input>

We will use the second assertion to remove the curvature of its Fukaya algebra. The first will then bound its degree-two endomorphisms. The geometric ingredient is a Stokes identity for disks with one interior marked point and no external boundary marked point. We construct that identity geometrically, then use filtered transfer to remove curvature.

===== 2.2.1 Branes, gradings, and the filtered construction <branes-gradings-and-the-filtered-construction>

Choose countably many Hamiltonian copies of the product test branes, ordered by the positive integers, with each test occurring cofinally. Use product Hamiltonian perturbations, small enough that the torus factors remain perturbed graphs. Arrange pairwise transversality and absence of triple intersections, including intersections with $L$. When both factors are present, impose these conditions separately on the factors of all embedded copies. Hamiltonian variations supply the required tangent directions, so these are countably many generic conditions.

Fix a split compatible almost complex structure $J_0$ with no nonconstant spheres and no nonconstant disks on any embedded test copy. The sphere and disk exclusion on the quartic factor is the genericity assertion used in the test-brane construction; on the torus factor it follows from the vanishing of the relevant second relative homotopy groups. No disk exclusion is imposed on $L$. All category constructions in this section involve a finite subcollection of the copies together with $L$.

Write $Lambda_0$ for the universal Novikov ring with complex coefficients and $Lambda$ for its fraction field; its monomials are written $T^lambda$, with valuation $lambda$. We use the immersed cyclic de Rham construction of Abouzaid–Fukaya–Oh–Ohta–Ono #cite(label("AFOOO2026")) Theorems 9.3 and 9.6. It gives a curved cyclic filtered $A_oo$ category with homotopy units for finite collections of closed spin immersions in a compact symplectic manifold. Here the relative spin background and bulk class are zero. The morphism spaces are forms on the source diagonals and the generators at transverse intersections. We denote the operations by $m_k$, their cyclic pairing by $chevron.l med \, med chevron.r$, and the curvature by $m_0$.

These operations have integer degree $2 - k$. Indeed, the brane gradings specify integer indices for the cap paths at every transverse end, including self-intersection ends. After capping, the boundary grading has Maslov index zero. There is no ambiguity from spheres because the ambient first Chern class vanishes. The same index calculation applies to parameterized operations. Energy monomials have degree zero, and all contractions, transfers, and comparison maps below are chosen homogeneous. Thus the grading is retained by the construction itself, including the shifted degrees or auxiliary variables used in a particular model.

We use Kuranishi structures with normalized boundaries and corners, outer collars where required, and continuous-family perturbations (CF perturbations). The local chart theorem and its evaluation submersion criterion are #cite(label("FOOOChartsI")) Theorem 7.1, Condition 7.11, and Lemma 7.12; compatible systems arise from disk-component-wise obstruction data #cite(label("FOOOChartsII")) Theorems 5.3 and 5.4. We use the corrected chart conventions of #cite(label("FOOOChartsICorrigendum")). The analytic gluing input is the exponential control of the gluing maps and their length derivatives #cite(label("FOOOGluing")) Assumption 3.12 and Theorems 3.13, 6.4, 8.16, and 8.17. For transverse immersed ends, the fixed-jump charts and spin determinant cappings are those of #cite(label("AkahoJoyce")) Sections 4–5, as incorporated in the immersed construction of #cite(label("AFOOO2026")).

In particular, the nodal chart uses component surjectivity augmented by matching equations at ordinary boundary nodes and by the transverse asymptotic conditions at jumps. Auxiliary markings stabilize the domains, and the obstruction spaces are transported on compact cores before gluing. These are the local inputs needed below when the total curve has no external boundary mark. All the additional spaces used for that purpose have one interior mark and no sphere components; there is no gluing at an angular interior node.

The construction is first made through finite energy and arity cutoffs. Filtered pseudo-isotopies, homotopy limits, and canonical models then give the complete category #cite(label("AFOOO2026")) Sections 6, 9–10, and 12, in particular Propositions 9.55 and 12.10. We keep $J_0$ fixed in this passage. The following observation gives a common energy filtration for a finite list of branes.

#thm([Lemma 2.6.])[
  (Energy values).  #emph[For a finite list of the immersed and embedded branes above, the energies of $J_0$-holomorphic polygons form a locally finite subset of $bb(R)_(gt.eq 0)$. In particular, the nonconstant polygons have a common positive energy lower bound, independent of their number of marked points.]
] <curv:energy>

#proof[
  Let $K subset D$ be the union of their images. It has the homotopy type of a finite complex and has arbitrarily small regular neighborhoods: away from the finitely many double points these are ordinary tubular neighborhoods, and at a double point they are the standard transverse-double neighborhoods. Relative de Rham theory therefore represents every class in $H^2 \( D \, K \; bb(R) \)$ by a smooth closed form that vanishes near $K$.
  The symplectic form has a relative lift that computes the polygon areas. To see this directly, construct near $K$ a primitive $alpha$ of $omega$ whose pullback to every sheet is zero. At a double point, simultaneous straightening of the transverse Lagrangians gives a radial primitive with this property. On the remaining pieces use the Lagrangian neighborhood theorem. After shrinking the neighborhoods, their overlaps at the ends of the pieces are collars on disjoint sheets. The differences of the primitives are exact there, with potentials zero on the sheets, by the relative Poincaré lemma. These potentials patch the primitives. Cutting off $alpha$ gives a closed form $tilde(omega)$ vanishing near $K$ and representing the relative lift. Stokes’ theorem, with the usual decay at jump ends, gives
  $ integral u^(*) tilde(omega) = integral u^(*) omega $
  for every polygon $u$.
  Choose closed forms vanishing near $K$ that represent a basis of $H^2 \( D \, K \; bb(R) \)$. Their integrals over a $J_0$-holomorphic polygon are bounded in absolute value by fixed constants times its area: this is the pointwise comass estimate for the compatible metric. A polygon defines an integral class in $H_2 \( D \, K \; bb(Z) \)$, whose quotient by torsion is a lattice. The preceding bounds leave only finitely many lattice points below a fixed area cutoff. Pairing with $\[ tilde(omega) \]$ leaves only finitely many energies. Energy positivity now gives the asserted lower bound whenever a nonconstant polygon exists. If none exists, any positive lower bound will do. ◻
]

The additive monoid generated by these energies is locally finite as well: below an energy bound $B$, each summand belongs to a finite set and a sum has at most $B \/ delta$ positive summands, where $delta > 0$ is the gap. This is the filtration used for the transfer operations.

At energy zero, the unary operation is the de Rham differential on the source diagonals and zero on transverse-point generators; the constant strip with no stabilizing mark is unstable. Transfer at energy zero consequently gives the graded canonical space

$ C_L^(upright("can")) = H^(*) \( L \; bb(C) \) ⊗ Lambda_0 #h(0em) xor #h(-1em) #h(-1em) xor.big_(x upright(" a branch generator")) #h(-1em) Lambda_0 x . quad "(2.10)" $ <curv:canonical-space>

The diagonal transfer can be made cyclic using a compatible de Rham contraction. Homotopy units will give cohomological units after curvature is removed. Since every branch generator has odd degree, the degree-two part of this canonical space is exactly $H^2 \( L \; bb(C) \) ⊗ Lambda_0$. Removing curvature will therefore give the cohomological bound in the following proposition, which is the output of this section.

#thm([Proposition 2.7.])[
  #emph[For every finite subcollection of the embedded test copies, $L$ and those copies define an uncurved proper cohomologically unital cyclic $A_oo$ category over $Lambda$. Its split-closed triangulated hull has Serre functor $\[ n \]$, and 
$ dim_Lambda "Hom"^2 \( L \, L \) lt.eq b_2 \( L \) = b_2 \( D \) - dim_(bb(Q)) cal(Z) . quad "(2.11)" $ <curv:degree-two-bound>

 For a sequence of embedded copies with strictly increasing labels, the operations are the geometric disk operations at $J_0$, up to filtered pseudo-isotopy through every finite cutoff.]
] <prop:curvature>

The proof occupies the rest of the section. To remove curvature, we construct scalar operations on disks with an interior root, relative to the ordinary cyclic disk operations. Their Stokes identity detects the leading curvature class against ambient cohomology and remains valid after positive-valuation boundary insertions. Filtered transfer then gives the proposition.

===== 2.2.2 A Stokes identity with one interior mark <a-stokes-identity-with-one-interior-mark>

Fix the collared componentwise Kuranishi data and CF-perturbations defining the cyclic boundary-rooted disk operations through a finite energy and arity cutoff, enlarged to contain the factors of all boundary strata that will occur. We construct additional scalar operations relative to these geometric choices. Their disks carry one distinguished interior mark and cyclically ordered boundary inputs, but no boundary output. The component containing the interior mark roots a broken disk tree. Every other component has its ordinary boundary output at the node pointing toward that root.

Let $eta$ be a closed smooth $\( n - 2 \)$-form on $D$. Pulling back $eta$ at the interior mark and the inputs at the boundary marks, and integrating, gives functionals

$ p_k^eta \( x_1 \, dots.h \, x_k \) = sum_beta T^(omega \( beta \)) p_(k \, beta)^eta \( x_1 \, dots.h \, x_k \) \, #h(2em) k gt.eq 1 . quad "(2.12)" $ <curv:bulk-derivative>

We include all words of diagonal and transverse branch sectors, with their capping orientation lines. The signs use the suspended degrees $\| x \|' = \| x \| - 1$. Thus a cyclic permutation of the inputs has its usual Koszul sign in these suspended degrees. On a continuous-lift sector the virtual dimension of the underlying disk problem is

$ "vdim" cal(P)_(k \, beta) = n + k - 1 . quad "(2.13)" $ <curv:unmarked-dimension>

The capped index gives the corresponding formula on a word containing branch sectors. The operation is zero unless its integrand has top degree. Spaces with $k = 0$ are used for Stokes; the unstable constant disk with just the interior mark is excluded.

#thm([Lemma 2.8.])[
  (Interior-rooted virtual integration).  #emph[Relative to the fixed cyclic boundary-rooted operations, these spaces admit oriented virtual integrations through every finite cutoff. Their normalized boundary products have an interior-rooted parent and an ordinary boundary-rooted child, matched either over the source diagonal or at reciprocal branch sectors. The integrations are cyclic in their external inputs and obey Stokes and composition. In particular, 
$ p_1^eta \( m_0 \) = 0 \, #h(2em) p_(1 \, 0)^eta \( c \) = plus.minus integral_L c and i^(*) eta quad \( c in Omega^2 \( L \) \) . quad "(2.14)" $ <curv:pairing-identity>

 The sign in the second identity is fixed and nonzero. The ordinary disk operations retain their chosen values through the cutoff.]
] <curv:stokes>

#proof[
  We construct the interior-rooted spaces with all immersed boundary labels. Their local boundary problems are the ordinary diagonal and transverse-jump problems of #cite(label("AkahoJoyce")) Sections 4–5; the relative construction below specifies their organization when no boundary output is chosen. It uses the mixed parent–child organization of #cite(label("AFOOO2026")) Propositions 16.3 and 16.14, retaining reciprocal internal switches even when the external word is empty. The parent is always additional interior-rooted data. Its identification with a cyclic boundary-rooted operation is neither required nor imposed.
  #emph[Compactification and normalization.] Keep the interior mark in immersed stable-map compactness. At a boundary node the two lifts either agree in $L$ or end at distinct preimages $p \, q$ of a double value. In the latter case the two normalized flags have the reciprocal labels $\( p \, q \)$ and $\( q \, p \)$. These flags disappear from the external word on gluing; they remain part of the normalized stratum. In particular, an empty external word does not restrict its compactification to the diagonal sector.
  There are no nonconstant spheres. A connected tree of constant spheres attached at one interior node and carrying at most the one interior mark cannot be stable: it has fewer than three external special points in total. The degeneration in which the entire boundary collapses would retain a nonconstant sphere, and is also absent. All remaining strata are disk trees. Constant stable vertices are retained, including the disk with the interior mark and one boundary node. The constant disk with no boundary input or node and only the interior mark is unstable and gives no stratum.
