==== 2.3 Comparison with the ordinary factor categories <sec:comparison>

Proposition 29 places the immersion and the embedded test branes in a proper uncurved cyclic category. To use the ordinary mirror equivalences, we must identify the category generated by the embedded tests inside that category. The needed comparison concerns only words of distinct, ordered copies. This restriction gives isolated asymptotic intersections at every end, including every internal end of a broken polygon, and removes both self-intersection sectors and unit conventions from the analytic comparison.

Fix one of the finite full cyclic categories constructed in Proposition 29, and select its embedded copy labels. Choose any total ordering of these labels and write the copies in that order as

$ K_a = Sigma_a times G_a \, #h(2em) 1 lt.eq a lt.eq N \, $

where each is a copy of a test from Proposition 2. Omit $Sigma_a$ when $Y$ is a point. Here $Sigma_a$ and $G_a$ include the Hamiltonian perturbations and transported brane structures of the copies. As in the preceding construction, all pairs are transverse and there are no triple intersections, also in each factor separately. The fixed split almost complex structure $J_0$ has no nonconstant spheres, and none of these embedded copies bounds a nonconstant $J_0$-holomorphic disk. A #emph[strict word] means a sequence of labels $a_0 < dots.h.c < a_k$. Its inputs lie in the intersection complexes for $\( K_(a_(i - 1)) \, K_(a_i) \)$, and its output lies in the complex for $\( K_(a_0) \, K_(a_k) \)$. We initially omit identities and self-morphism spaces. Thus the directed category in this section means precisely these complexes and their operations on strict words.

#thm([Proposition 2.10.])[
  (Directed comparison).  #emph[ For each fixed finite full cyclic category above and every total ordering of its embedded copy labels, its strict directed sector is quasi-isomorphic, after an extension of its Novikov field if necessary, to the corresponding ordinary directed Fukaya category of the embedded product copies. When $Y$ is present, this ordinary directed category is quasi-isomorphic to the directed restriction of the $A_oo$ tensor product of the two ordinary factor categories. These comparisons use the specified brane labels and their usual graded intersection complexes. Consequently the ordinary mirror functors in the two factors give a comparison with the directed category on the specified objects $Q_ell times.square N_M$. All the finitely many ordering comparisons for this same full cyclic category may be made over one field extension.]
] <prop:directed-comparison>

The two comparisons change different parts of the construction. For the first, we keep $J_0$ and the intersection generators fixed and change disk integration into regular Floer counts by introducing Hamiltonian terms. Section 3.3 verifies the relative chart and gluing hypotheses for that change. It then removes the energy cutoffs algebraically, while retaining the directed sector of the originally fixed full cyclic category.

For the second comparison, we retain the regular counts and change their domain parameters. The product category uses the same polygon in both factors; the tensor product uses an associahedral diagonal. Sections 3.4–3.6 construct and count a homotopy between these choices, including the boundary and sign identities that make the counts an operad map. Section 3.7 then identifies the result with the tensor product of the ordinary factor categories. All these comparisons concern embedded copies, and ordinary mirror symmetry is used in the individual factors. The freedom to order the same copies in every way will allow us to test compositions through three distinct copies and prove that the copy arrows are invertible in Lemma 36.

===== 2.3.1 Regular Hamiltonian models with fixed intersections <comp:regular-models>

For each ordered pair choose strip data of the form $H \( t \, x \) thin d t$, $0 lt.eq t lt.eq 1$, with $H$ small and identically zero on fixed small balls around the intersections of that pair. Take $H$ sufficiently small on the complement as well. Its chords are then exactly the original intersections. On a stable polygon, with at least two inputs, use a domain-dependent Hamiltonian one-form whose tangential restriction to every Lagrangian boundary arc is zero. The almost complex structure remains $J_0$. Product data are the sums of the corresponding data in the two factors.

We require agreement with the pair data on the terminal parts of the external ends and on long necks. On polygon cores the data are smooth up to domain faces and restrict there to the data on the component polygons. More precisely, use the usual consistent strip-like ends and simultaneous plumbing collars on the compactified domain spaces. If $T$ is a neck length, smoothness of the core coefficients is taken in a coordinate $exp \( - c T \)$, for a fixed $c > 0$. Thus their difference from the limiting component coefficients, together with every required length derivative, is exponentially small.

These conditions leave enough freedom for regularity. To see their compatibility, first choose data agreeing with their component data on smaller collars and carrying the prescribed strip model near each node. Retain that strip model on terminal end and neck neighborhoods; variations occur on the complementary core patches. The restrictions to intersecting domain faces agree, so extend their coefficients by the ordinary extension theorem for functions on a manifold with corners, using inclusion–exclusion and a partition of unity. The zero tangential boundary condition is linear and is preserved by this extension. At later arities one may shrink the fixed neighborhoods, since the lower-arity data already equal the strip model there. This construction gives smooth extensions with equality on faces; it does not impose equality with component data on an entire domain collar. In particular, at any smooth domain, even one lying in a plumbing collar, there remain open interior core patches on which the Hamiltonian data may vary. The radial plumbing description and strip height used here are also those of #cite(label("A8")) Proposition 7.5.

#thm([Lemma 2.11.])[
  #emph[For the finite list above, the Hamiltonian data can be chosen small, with the stated face and strip conditions, so that the ordinary strict-word operations are regular. They can also be chosen regular over any prescribed countable collection of smooth parameter chains in products of domain spaces, including all their relative face interiors and all component configurations at those faces. Compactness below an area bound has only polygon and strip degenerations. All nonconstant strips and all contributing stable polygons have positive area, with a common positive lower bound for this finite directed system.]
] <comp:regularity>

#proof[
  The energy identity compares analytic energy with ordinary symplectic area and a curvature term supported on the cores. The integral of that curvature is uniformly bounded for each word, because the pure strip model has zero curvature on every neck. There is no boundary term along a labeled arc, and the chords have not moved. On strips, analytic energy equals area. Areas are additive at broken configurations and are constant in a relative class during the allowed homotopies of data.
  At a small-scale bubble the Hamiltonian terms disappear, leaving a $J_0$-holomorphic sphere or a disk on one of the embedded copies. Neither exists. Compactness therefore reduces to ordinary Floer compactness with isolated transverse asymptotes. Below a fixed area bound, only finitely many relative classes are realized, even before regularity: near a limiting broken configuration the total relative class is fixed. For regular data this gives finite zero-dimensional counts and compactifications of the one-dimensional moduli spaces.
  Nonconstant strips have positive area and a positive energy gap. Stable polygons also have a uniform positive area gap once the data are sufficiently small. Otherwise a sequence of data tending to zero and polygons with areas tending to a nonpositive value would have analytic energies tending to zero. Compactness, including on long necks, would make every component constant. A stable vertex in a strict word has at least three distinct boundary labels, so such a constant component would give a triple intersection. This is excluded. There are only finitely many strict words, which gives a single small-data neighborhood and a common gap.
  For strips, regularity follows by varying $H \( t \, x \)$. The regular-point argument based on unique continuation gives dense injective points of $\( t \, u \( s \, t \) \)$ on every nonconstant trajectory, away from its asymptotes; it applies with Lagrangian boundary just as in the Floer strip argument of #cite(label("FloerHoferSalamon")), with the corrected linear-algebra step in #cite(label("FloerHoferSalamonErratum")). The required variations may be supported away from the intersection balls. Indeed a nonconstant strip cannot remain entirely in those balls: the local sheets are exact there, so its area would be zero. A constant strip has an invertible transverse operator.
  For polygons, domain-dependent Hamiltonian variations on interior core patches give transversality without a simplicity assumption on the map. The same argument applies over any fixed smooth parameter chain. On a domain face, the vertices in one factor have different tuples of boundary labels, by strictness. Their variations can therefore be chosen independently. A variation on the relative interior of a face extends to the full system by the face-extension construction above; the restrictions on higher families do not constrain variations on these component core patches. Thus the universal linearized problem is surjective on every relevant stratum. Sard–Smale, with countable exhaustions by energy, class, and parameter stratum, supplies simultaneous regularity. Applied to the diagonal chains it gives regular product polygon operations. Product strip regularity is the direct-sum statement for the factor strip operators. ◻
]

===== 2.3.2 Relation to the ordinary Fukaya categories <comp:ordinary>

The preceding data compute the ordinary factor categories used in mirror symmetry. For the graphs this is ordinary weakly exact Floer theory. For the quartic spheres, take their disk-excluding almost complex structure to be $J_0$, as permitted in Seidel’s construction #cite(label("SeidelQuartic")) Section 8. It is also sphere-free, so regularity at these fixed data needs no additional bubble-incidence condition. Extend the strict-word data to all ordinary words on the same copies; repeated labels use nondegenerate chord perturbations. The extension is again inductive over domain faces, with the same terminal strip models and smooth core coefficients.

We explain why the slightly more flexible collar condition used above gives the same ordinary category. One can put two choices on separately labeled copies and extend their mixed-word data. Energy and compactness for the comparison follow from the same no-bubbling argument. At a broken curve the core coefficients differ from their limits exponentially in the neck lengths, so ordinary gluing with isolated asymptotes applies. Continuation, binary products, and self-Floer units then identify the two labels of each object. This is the ordinary comparison argument; the collar proof in #cite(label("A8")) Proposition 7.5 uses exactly these hypotheses and even allows decaying errors in strip-end coefficients.

When comparing with an ordinary choice that has varying almost complex data, the quartic factor requires the usual low-dimensional sphere-avoidance argument. The prescribed disk-excluding structure at each boundary label is retained, which excludes boundary bubbles. In real dimension four with $c_1 = 0$, a simple unmarked sphere has expected dimension $- 2$. Adding the two incidence marks, one on the sphere and one on the principal component, and imposing the four-dimensional matching condition leaves a loss of two dimensions. Removing a sphere tree does not change the principal component index. Hence sphere incidence can be avoided in the zero- and one-dimensional systems used for operations and comparisons. For a multiply covered sphere, its image lies on a simple sphere, so the same incidence exclusion suffices.

The required transversality uses almost complex variations for the sphere and Hamiltonian variations for the principal component, including evaluation at the incidence point. Constant or contracted components do not alter this test. In a relative construction, previously fixed faces already satisfy the exclusion, and the new variations are available on the relative interiors. The terminal strip and neck data may be chosen sphere-free; strip data depending on strip height form only a one-parameter family. Continuation data vary on compact cores, where the same argument applies. These are the sphere-exclusion and comparison mechanisms of #cite(label("SeidelQuartic")) Section 8. Thus the mixed-copy construction identifies our regular systems with conventional ordinary systems, including cohomological units. The actual Hamiltonian sphere copies represent the original objects by #cite(label("SeidelQuartic")) Lemma 8.14.

If one uses capped-generator Novikov exponents in the rational-brane normalization, rather than ordinary polygon areas, the coefficients differ by fixed input and output scalar factors. Rescaling the generator lines after the permitted scalar extension makes these descriptions agree. This completes the identification of the regular factor models. It remains to compare them with the disk integration operations that were used to include the immersion.

===== 2.3.3 The relative comparison from disk integration <comp:virtual>

Join the zero Hamiltonian data to the regular data by a small path, constant near its endpoints. Keep $J_0$ and every intersection chord fixed. We use the disk Kuranishi and CF-perturbation construction over this path. Here a CF perturbation is the compatible finite dimensional perturbation used to integrate along a Kuranishi correspondence. The comparison will use its relative extension and Stokes properties; it does not require cyclic perturbations along the path.

#thm([Lemma 2.12.])[
  #emph[For every finite energy and arity cutoff, the strict-word disk integration operations at the zero end and the ordinary regular counts at the other end are related by a filtered pseudo-isotopy. The linear comparison has the identity reduction on the intersection generators. The data at either endpoint may be recovered up to the usual finite-cutoff pseudo-isotopy.]
] <comp:pseudoisotopy>

#proof[
  We verify that the relative disk construction applies to the Hamiltonian terms just introduced. The analytic point is that the unchanged transverse asymptotes and the controlled core coefficients give uniform gluing estimates at every corner. With these estimates, the cited chart and relative perturbation constructions apply with the path parameter included. The inputs are the disk charts from obstruction bundle data, their compatible products on normalized corners, and the relative CF extension and Stokes theorems used in the cyclic construction #cite(label("AFOOO2026")) Sections 9–10 and 12. For the chart and gluing theorems, we use #cite(label("FOOOChartsI")) Theorem 7.1, Condition 7.11, and Lemma 7.12, the disk-component-wise compatibility construction of #cite(label("FOOOChartsII")), and the exponential gluing estimates in #cite(label("FOOOGluing")) Assumption 3.12 and Theorems 3.13, 6.4, 8.16–8.17. The following verifications explain their application to this relative problem.
  ======= 2.3.3.1 Charts and the path parameter. <charts-and-the-path-parameter.>
  Use stabilized strip and polygon domains, with the path coordinate included among the parameters. The Hamiltonian terms on a core are smooth lower-order terms in the Cauchy–Riemann equation. Compact-core obstruction sections, transported and summed as in the disk-chart construction, make the linearized equation surjective at a fixed domain and fixed path parameter. Interior test sections suffice: the adjoint unique-continuation argument is unchanged by these smooth lower-order terms. This also makes the path projection submersive. Strip translation is removed by the usual local slice. When stabilization uses auxiliary domain points constrained to target slices, suitable immersive points exist: the maps under consideration have strictly positive area. Strict words give no map isotropy, and the no-bubbling argument requires no sphere chart.
  ======= 2.3.3.2 Coefficients near broken configurations. <coefficients-near-broken-configurations.>
  At a gluing seam the ends lie in the balls where the Hamiltonian vanishes. The seam equation therefore coincides with the unperturbed transverse-end equation. A nonconstant strip core down a neck may carry a Hamiltonian term, but that term is translation invariant and transports smoothly with the core.
  There is a coordinate issue when a domain neck contains several Floer breaking seams. Its domain length is the total length of the intervening pieces. Consequently its exponential plumbing coordinate is a product of the exponential seam coordinates, times a smooth positive factor. The core coefficients retain their smoothness and exponential derivative estimates after this contraction. Equivalently, in stabilized charts keep the auxiliary marks that locate all the retained cores.
  For completeness, use radial variables
  $ r_l = e^(- pi s) \, #h(2em) r_r = e^(- pi \( T - s \)) $
  on a boundary neck, together with the strip height. Transport to a stabilized strip core multiplies each one-sided radial variable by the intervening radial factors and smooth core factors. Changes of boundary-node coordinate have a positive nonzero leading real coefficient. An origin shift of order $r_l r_r$, after division by one radial variable, still contains the opposite radial factor. The strip coframe thus tends smoothly to the limiting strip coframe in these coordinates. These are the radial collar calculations in #cite(label("A8")) proof of Proposition 7.5. For coordinate changes between map charts one retains the marks needed for local stability, including marks on strip components. There is no requirement to express the inverse total neck length smoothly in the inverse lengths of a contracted chain.
  ======= 2.3.3.3 Uniform gluing estimates. <uniform-gluing-estimates.>
  Choose exponential weights below the spectral gaps of the finitely many transverse asymptotic operators. On a joined strip of length $T$, a smoothed version of $e^(delta min \( s \, T - s \))$ gives a suitable weight. Augment the component operators by kernel observables on compact cores and by the obstruction variables. Pregluing errors are exponentially small in a slightly weaker weight: the maps decay at the ends, the seam equations agree, and the core coefficient errors are exponentially small.
  To build a parametrix, split the forcing near each seam midpoint in a fixed-width overlap, solve with the augmented component inverses, and splice over intervals whose lengths are proportional to $T$. The component one-sided weights dominate the joined weight on their respective sides, uniformly on that overlap. The parametrix error is the sum of an $O \( T^(- 1) \)$ cutoff error and an exponentially small coefficient error. There is no matching zero mode at a transverse intersection. A Neumann-series correction therefore gives a uniform augmented inverse; the Fredholm indices agree under gluing. The quadratic estimate is uniform as well, since all new lower-order terms occur on fixed compact cores and the seam equation near the joined values is unchanged.
  The differentiated estimates have the same form. Core length derivatives of coefficients are exponentially small. Derivatives of middle cutoffs and reparametrizations multiply exponentially decaying map terms. Passing to weaker weights accommodates every fixed finite number of derivatives, for several necks and with some nodes still broken. Hence the usual disk-chart smoothness in inverse-length corner coordinates applies. These are precisely the exponential estimates required by the cited FOOO gluing theorems. Alternatively, the isolated-end case of #cite(label("A8")) Lemma 4.11 and Proposition 4.12 uses the same augmented surjectivity, spectral gaps, core transports, and exponentially controlled coefficients. Its strip splice estimates are estimates for finite-rank systems and apply in the present dimension. The converse gluing assertion follows from closeness on the cores and decay along the joining ends, giving the local chart coverage.
  ======= 2.3.3.4 Compatible charts and relative perturbations. <compatible-charts-and-relative-perturbations.>
  At a normalized corner, choose the product of the component charts over their common path parameter, summing their core-supported obstruction data. At every iterated corner the limiting equation data agree by the face construction. The disk-component-wise compatibility procedure now uses the same stabilizations, nested neighborhoods, transported core sections, openness of augmented surjectivity, and obstruction-data enlargements as for unforced disks. Compactness gives a finite construction at each cutoff. The preceding estimates supply the inverse-function and gluing steps, with the path parameter included. Thus the smooth lower-order core terms introduce no new condition in that construction. There are no forgetful-map or cyclic compatibility requirements on strict words, and all seam matches are at points.
  Use the usual outer collars and relative CF extension, preserving the component products and the submersive path projection. Stokes then gives the pseudo-isotopy equations with the required face products. Endpoint chart choices need only be recovered up to a finite-cutoff pseudo-isotopy, as in the ordinary relative construction. At the regular endpoint, a virtual zero-dimensional moduli space has no boundary strata and is already an oriented zero-manifold without isotropy. Its Kuranishi integral is its ordinary signed count, also if one uses a chart with redundant obstruction directions. Thus the endpoint operations are the ordinary operations from Lemma 33.
