All orientations in this comparison come from the same graded spin determinant lines, with zero background class. The comparison of sign conventions is proved in Section 3.6 below. If an ordinary convention reverses arrows, use the opposite category and the corresponding algebraic duality; no reflection of holomorphic disks is involved.

We next remove the cutoffs without imposing simultaneous geometric choices at all orders. Let $Lambda$ be the Novikov field in use, with valuation $v$. There are finitely many strict words in the finite list, and every intersection complex is finite dimensional. Thus the equations for an $A_oo$ isomorphism between its two directed structures are a finite polynomial system over $Lambda$. Include variables for the inverse of its linear part. For any valuation bound, Lemma 34, together with the finite-cutoff comparisons of the chosen promoted cyclic model, provides an approximate solution. Its coordinates have nonnegative valuation, as do the inverse linear-part coordinates, because that linear part has invertible reduction. In particular, the source of these comparisons is the restriction of the fixed full cyclic category. Changing chart or perturbation choices for a particular ordering is accounted for by these endpoint pseudo-isotopies; it does not replace that ambient category.

If this finite system had no solution over an algebraic closure of $Lambda$, the Nullstellensatz would give a fixed polynomial combination of its equations equal to $1$. Evaluation at an approximate solution of sufficiently large valuation contradicts this identity: all polynomial coefficients have a fixed lower valuation bound, whereas the equation errors have arbitrarily high valuation. A solution therefore exists after field extension. The argument applies to every ordering of the same finite label set. There are only finitely many orderings, so all their polynomial solutions may be chosen over one algebraically closed extension of the original coefficient field. This proves the first assertion of Proposition 32. We now retain the regular ordinary models and compare their product operations.

===== 2.3.4 Chains of polygon domains <comp:domain-chains>

Write $cal(K)_j$ for the compactified space of disk domains with $j$ inputs and one output, $j gt.eq 2$. It has dimension $j - 2$ and its faces are products of lower polygon spaces. We use its usual smooth face structure from strip-like ends and plumbing. A pair of factor polygons has domain parameter in $cal(K)_j times cal(K)_j$. The product Fukaya operation uses the diagonal copy of $cal(K)_j$ in this product. Replacing this diagonal by a cellular operad diagonal will give the tensor product.

We need a chain model in which this replacement can be counted geometrically. A geometric chain in $cal(K)_j^2$ is a smooth map

$ c : P arrow.r cal(K)_j^2 $

from a compact oriented smooth manifold with corners and finitely many faces. Every relative face interior of $P$, including its interior, must map into a relative face interior of $cal(K)_j^2$. Impose also the following condition on the way target faces are left. If a face of $P$ has codimension one in a containing face, the inward derivative of $c$ is strictly inward at every target face hypersurface that contains the smaller image interior but does not contain the larger image interior. Derivatives here are taken in the exponential plumbing coordinates. The condition allows several target nodes to be smoothed by one inward parameter.

Take linear combinations of these maps, with the usual orientation, reparametrization, and disjoint-union relations; the boundary is the sum over oriented codimension-one faces. A map that ignores a direct product factor of positive dimension is declared zero. This is a subcomplex relation: if the ignored factor has dimension one, its signed endpoints cancel, and in larger dimensions its boundary factors are still ignored. Products, face inclusions, diagonals, and grafting in both target coordinates preserve the face condition. With the identity in arity one adjoined, these chains form a nonsymmetric differential graded operad, denoted $cal(G)$; a chain of dimension $d$ has cohomological degree $- d$.

#thm([Lemma 2.13.])[
  #emph[In every arity $j gt.eq 2$, the chain complex $cal(G) \( j \)$ has the homology of a point, with the ordinary augmentation in degree zero.]
] <comp:chain-acyclic>

#proof[
  Choose a smooth homotopy of $cal(K)_j^2$ that immediately moves every boundary point into the interior, with strictly inward initial derivative at all its incident hypersurfaces, and then contracts within the interior. Such a homotopy is obtained from an inward vector field followed by a contraction of a compact subset of the open cell. Apply it to a chain and use its prism.
  At time zero the strict inward condition gives exactly the required condition on the new prism faces. At positive time the image is in the target interior, so no further inward condition remains. The prism identity is therefore valid in $cal(G)$. At the constant endpoint every positive-dimensional chain ignores its whole domain as a product factor and is zero. A zero-dimensional chain is reduced to its augmentation times the chosen interior point. This gives the asserted contraction. ◻
]

We can now choose the domain homotopy, before choosing the Floer data with which it will be counted. Let $cal(A)_oo = C_(upright("cell")) \( cal(K)_bullet \)$ be the cellular dg operad of associahedra, with the same cohomological convention. Choose a cellular diagonal

$ Delta : cal(A)_oo arrow.r cal(A)_oo ⊗ cal(A)_oo quad "(2.18)" $ <comp:cellular-diagonal>

lifting multiplication. Such a diagonal exists by induction on arity, or by the free resolution description; see #cite(label("MarklShnider")). No coassociative choice is required.

There are two maps from $cal(A)_oo$ to $cal(G)$. The first takes the fundamental chain of $cal(K)_j$ to its actual diagonal in $cal(K)_j^2$. The second applies $Delta$ and then the cross product of geometric fundamental chains. Both preserve the augmentation. Lemma 35 and cofibrancy of the $A_oo$ operad imply that they are dg-operad homotopic. One can construct the homotopy arity by arity, using polynomial differential forms on an interval as a path object: each obstruction is an augmentation-zero cycle in an acyclic arity.

Fix these two maps and one such homotopy in $cal(G)$. Their coefficients involve only countably many geometric chains. Take the dg suboperad $cal(G)_0 subset cal(G)$ generated by those chains, and include the representatives and chain relations used in this construction among the choices below. The countable family remains countable after adjoining products, faces, and maps with ignored factors removed. If a relation requires additional representatives, add finite witnesses for a countable basis of the relation space and repeat this closure. This still uses only countably many maps. Choose regular split Hamiltonian data for this family by Lemma 33. We require counts only on $cal(G)_0$; no assertion of its acyclicity is needed.

When a parameter lies in a target face, count the polygons at all its vertices, matching the internal ends and summing over their discrete intersection generators. Weight a configuration by its total area. The fixed-domain Fredholm index is

$ \| x_0 \| - sum_(i = 1)^j \| x_i \| . $

Consequently a rigid count over a $d$-dimensional parameter chain acts in degree $- d$.

===== 2.3.5 Compactification, gluing, and the chain identities <comp:chain-counts>

We show that these parametric counts, with the orientation normalization specified in the next subsection, define a dg operad map from $cal(G)_0$ to the endomorphism operations of the directed intersection complexes. This is the geometric assertion needed for the diagonal replacement. It consists of compatibility with the ignored-factor relation, boundary, and grafting.

If a chain ignores a positive-dimensional product factor, a zero-dimensional count on it would have negative expected dimension after that factor is removed. Include the maps with those factors removed among the regularity requirements. Their moduli spaces are empty, so the ignored-factor relation is respected.

Consider next a one-dimensional parametric moduli space below a fixed area bound. Compactness in Lemma 33 permits two phenomena: approach to a face of $P$ and breaking of a Floer strip. Passing to a codimension-$r$ face of $P$ lowers the virtual dimension by $r$. This remains true when the face creates several nodes in one or both polygon factors. Indeed the fixed-domain indices add under matching at isolated transverse intersections; the domain parameters are supplied by $P$. Each additional nonconstant quotient strip lowers dimension by one more. Stratum regularity therefore excludes a strip breaking over a parameter boundary in a one-dimensional problem, including a breaking down any newly formed neck. The same argument excludes boundary strata in a zero-dimensional problem. Repeated labels on strip components cause no additional fiber-product transversality requirement, since every match is discrete.

The uniform curvature bounds apply while a chain parameter tends to a face. Thus this is compactness for the parametric problem, not merely compactness at each fixed parameter. Its limiting configurations at a target face are exactly the component polygons and the strip breakings just described.

We must also know that each parameter-face configuration is the end of the one-dimensional moduli space with its prescribed orientation. Use a local collar $\( t \, y \)$ on $P$, with $t gt.eq 0$ increasing inward. For every newly smoothed neck its plumbing coordinate has the form

$ lambda_i \( t \, y \) = t thin c_i \( t \, y \) \, #h(2em) c_i \( t \, y \) > 0 \, quad "(2.19)" $ <comp:simultaneous-plumbing>

by the face condition. The neck lengths tend to infinity, and their derivatives in $y$ stay bounded. At $t = 0$, regularity of the nodal problem includes the $y$ variables. Its augmented linear operator is therefore the invertible operator needed for the rigid parametric configuration.

For fixed small $t > 0$, simultaneously preglue all these necks and apply the weighted inverse construction from Lemma 34, including the $y$ variables in the linearization. Core coefficients converge to their limiting values. Differentiating in $y$ introduces only bounded length derivatives multiplied by core-supported terms or by terms decaying along the necks. On the component halves, the length-dependent transport has the same exponential estimates. The implicit function theorem gives a unique glued solution for each sufficiently small $t > 0$, and ordinary gluing coverage identifies all solutions near the nodal configuration with this end. Transport of the determinant orientation gives the boundary orientation, with $t$ increasing inward.

This argument uses oriented-end coverage and the inverse estimates for $t > 0$. It does not assert that the solution map is smooth at $t = 0$ in exponential plumbing coordinates. The disk charts used in Lemma 34 have the usual inverse-length smoothness; these two uses of gluing require different conclusions.

At an edge already internal in a target face, a broken strip can occur as an output breaking of the child polygon or as an input breaking of the parent. These are the same matched configurations with opposite boundary signs, so they cancel. External strip breakings give the Hom differential. Grafting of chains gives composition by the same discrete matching rule. Finally, on a product of chains in the individual domain spaces, the count factors: regularity makes a rigid product configuration a product of rigid configurations in the two factors. The remaining point is that all these identities use a single consistent sign convention.

===== 2.3.6 Determinant lines and signs <comp:orientations>

We use the graded orientation lines of Floer generators and the spin determinant capping and gluing theorem, with zero background class; for the immersed conventions see #cite(label("AkahoJoyce")) Sections 4–5. Our factor Lagrangian dimensions are even. This parity removes the extra signs that would come from moving the oriented rank contribution of a capped disk.

For a generator $x$, choose an input cap of index $\| x \|$ and an output cap of reciprocal index. Their parities agree in even dimension. Let $o_x$ be the input-cap line. Order a fixed map operator first, its input caps in order next, and its output cap last. The spin orientation of the resulting capped disk identifies the map determinant symbol with the graded Hom line

$ "Hom" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] o_(x_1) ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ o_(x_j) \, o_(x_0) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

Pair reciprocal cap lines so that the invertible transverse operator on a constant strip gives the identity of $o_x$.

The determinant gluing comparison at a specified end, with its length held fixed, is local at that end after these caps are inserted. Determinant excision detaches the outer framing and disk contributions; sphere determinants are complex oriented. Only the two node caps remain in the comparison. We record the permutation that recovers the usual Hom sign. Let a child map determinant have parity $b$, let the sum of the preceding parent input-cap parities be $l$, and let the node cap have parity $q$. The sum of the child input-cap parities is $b + q$. Starting with the parent and child determinants, the external input caps, the parent output cap, and the reciprocal node pair, reorder so that each determinant is followed by its own caps. The permutation exponent is

$ b l + q . $

For example, if $r$ is the sum of trailing parent input-cap parities and $o$ is its output-cap parity, the exponent before simplification is

$ b \( l + q + r + o \) + q \( b + q + r + o \) + \( r + o \) \( b + q \) = b l + q quad upright("in ") bb(Z) \/ 2 . $

The term depending only on the node, including $q$, is absorbed by the reciprocal contraction already normalized on the constant strip. Fixed-operator gluing therefore gives Hom composition with Koszul exponent $b l$. This calculation applies to formal Fredholm capping and gluing, independently of the existence of a rigid holomorphic curve.

For direct sums choose the product cap to be the direct sum of the factor caps, with line $o_x ⊗ o_y$. In the order $o_x ⊗ o_y ⊗ o_x^or ⊗ o_y^or$, its contraction first moves $o_y$ past $o_x^or$ and then applies the two factor contractions. The crossing contributes $\( - 1 \)^(\| x \| \| y \|)$, exactly the graded tensor-evaluation sign. The constant product strip is the direct sum of the constant factor strips, so its determinant unit is the tensor of their units and fixes this same reciprocal normalization. At a node the factors $\( - 1 \)^(q_x) \( - 1 \)^(q_y) = \( - 1 \)^(q_x + q_y)$ absorb the product node parity; the intervening mixed permutations remain precisely the tensor Hom signs. Determinant excision and product spin frames give this direct-sum identification, with no additional rank sign in our even dimensions.

For a parameter chain $c$ of dimension $d$, order its orientation before the map determinant and write $f \( c \)$ for the resulting raw count. Composing chains of dimensions $p$ and $q$ introduces $\( - 1 \)^(p q)$: the second parameter passes the first map determinant. The same sign appears for cross products. Define

$ gamma \( c \) = \( - 1 \)^(d \( d - 1 \) \/ 2) f \( c \) . quad "(2.20)" $ <comp:count-normalization>

The identity

$ frac(\( - 1 \)^(\( p + q \) \( p + q - 1 \) \/ 2) \( - 1 \)^(p q), \( - 1 \)^(p \( p - 1 \) \/ 2) \( - 1 \)^(q \( q - 1 \) \/ 2)) = 1 $

shows that $gamma$ respects both composition and cross products.

For the boundary identity let $v$ be the raw strip map with a fixed translation convention. An output strip places its map determinant before the polygon determinant, so its translation direction passes the $d$ parameter directions. Write $s in { 1 \, - 1 }$ for its comparison with outward-normal-first orientation before that permutation. At an input the strip translation has the opposite end direction, and its determinant follows the polygon determinant. Moving this translation past both the parameter and the rigid polygon determinant adds no further parity. Thus the oriented boundary equation is

$ f \( partial c \) + s \( - 1 \)^d v compose f \( c \) - s sum_i f \( c \) compose_i v = 0 \, quad "(2.21)" $ <comp:raw-boundary>

where the slot compositions already include their graded Hom signs. On product generators $v$ has the tensor differential rule.

The same calculation verifies internal-edge cancellation. Put the internal strip determinant in the same position on both descriptions of the broken configuration. Every permutation used to do this is also the permutation in Hom composition, by the fixed-operator calculation above. The two translation directions are opposite, which gives cancellation. At a parameter face, determinant gluing is compared at fixed positive smoothing values. The parameter orientations then compare by their ordinary boundary orientation, with $t$ inward in Equation (2.19). There is no separate normal sign for each node smoothed by that same $t$.

Set the Floer differential to be $s v$. Since $f \( c \)$ has degree $- d$, Equation (2.21) gives

$ d_(upright("End")) f \( c \) = \( - 1 \)^(d + 1) f \( partial c \) . $

The quotient of the normalization signs in dimensions $d$ and $d - 1$ is $\( - 1 \)^(d - 1)$. Therefore

$ d_(upright("End")) gamma \( c \) = gamma \( partial c \) . quad "(2.22)" $ <comp:dg-count>

Together with the compactification and grafting arguments, this proves the promised dg operad count identities.

We finally compare this convention with conventional Fukaya signs. Match the cap lines and the order of geometric inputs, using the same dg or suspended translation throughout. The remaining difference is removed by strict sign changes at generators, as in #cite(label("A8")) Lemma 7.7. Here is the argument in the present setting. Both conventions use the same spin orientation of the capped disk, the same complex orientation on sphere factors, and the same reciprocal contractions. They consequently have no relative-class-dependent ratio. At fixed index the transverse graded-plane data are connected; frame paths with the fixed spin data and determinant winding give the same conclusion. The remaining factors arise from order, marking, and suspension permutations and depend only on the corner indices.

To make the two-vertex argument explicit, use suspended degrees $a_i = \| x_i \| - 1$ and write the ratio as $\( - 1 \)^(r_k \( a_1 \, dots.h \, a_k \))$, with $r_k$ valued in $bb(Z) \/ 2$. The output suspended degree of a rigid vertex is $1 + sum_i a_i$. The determinant boundary identity says that

$ & r_s \( a_i \, dots.h \, a_(i + s - 1) \)\
 & quad + r_(k - s + 1) (a_1 \, dots.h \, a_(i - 1) \, 1 + sum_(j = i)^(i + s - 1) a_j \, a_(i + s) \, dots.h \, a_k) quad "(2.23)" $ <comp:sign-cocycle>

is independent of the inserted block and its length. Both conventions have the same suspended insertion sign. Thus Equation (2.23) compares the same oriented one-dimensional formal gluing problem. It applies to arbitrary integer cap indices, without assuming that the associated holomorphic moduli space is nonempty.

Choose $h : bb(Z) arrow.r bb(Z) \/ 2$ recursively so that $h \( a + 1 \) + h \( a \) = r_1 \( a \)$. Rescaling the line of suspended degree $a$ by $\( - 1 \)^(h \( a \))$ replaces $r_k$ by

$ r'_k = r_k + h (1 + sum_i a_i) + sum_i h \( a_i \) . $

It makes $r'_1 = 0$ and preserves Equation (2.23). Comparing a unary child at input $i$ with a unary parent now gives $r'_k \( dots.h \, a_i + 1 \, dots.h \) = r'_k \( dots.h \, a_i \, dots.h \)$. Thus $r'_k$ is a constant $b_k$. Comparing a binary child with a unary parent gives $b_k = b_2 + b_(k - 1)$, so $b_k = \( k - 1 \) b_2$. A common line rescaling by $\( - 1 \)^(b_2)$ removes these constants. This proves a strict sign identification for both the ordinary counts and the directed disk-integration comparison. The integer gradings and even factor dimensions used here are exactly the features needed in this argument.

===== 2.3.7 The operadic diagonal and the product comparison <comp:operadic-completion>

Apply the count map $gamma$ on $cal(G)_0$ to the homotopy chosen in Section 3.4. At the first endpoint, counts on the actual diagonal are the split product polygon counts. At the second endpoint, factorization on cross products gives precisely the tensor-product operations determined by $Delta$. For strips the two endpoints have the same tensor differential: a rigid quotient product strip consists of a rigid quotient strip in one factor and a constant strip in the other; if both factors move, the independent translation gives excess dimension.

The construction applies with object labels. The endomorphism operad simply has a component for each composable ordered word, and its compositions use the corresponding slots. Only the bounded arities allowed by the finite strict ordering occur. The operadic path therefore gives isomorphic directed $A_oo$ structures: integrate its coderivation homotopy successively in arity over the characteristic-zero field. The differential is fixed and there is no arity-one homotopy term, so every step is a finite integration of polynomial coefficients, with identity linear term.

To finish, the cellular-diagonal tensor computes the ordinary tensor through dg models and respects $A_oo$ quasi-isomorphisms. One may first forget units. Bar–cobar rectification replaces an $A_oo$ quasi-isomorphism by a zigzag of strict quasi-isomorphisms of operad algebras #cite(label("LodayVallette")). For fixed-object categories the same construction uses composable tensor words over the finite product of ground fields given by the object idempotents. Units may be adjoined freely when augmented resolutions are used. Tensor these strict diagrams with Equation (2.18); over a field their maps remain quasi-isomorphisms. At the dg endpoints only the associative multiplication survives, and the resulting tensor is the ordinary dg tensor. This also explains the agreement with the tensor-product framework in the product theorem of #cite(label("AmorimProduct")).

Restricting these comparisons to the product labels and strict words preserves every operation and functor equation used here. The ordinary factor mirror equivalences then give the stated directed comparison with their dg mirror categories. This proves the second assertion and completes Proposition 32.

==== 2.4 Completion of the realization construction <sec:realization>

We now prove Proposition 2. The immersion of Proposition 23 has been placed in a proper uncurved cyclic category by Proposition 29, and Proposition 32 identifies the directed operations on every finite collection of embedded copies with those on the corresponding mirror tests. We first assemble these finite comparisons. Cofinal copies will then recover the full mirror category, to which the immersed object admits an orthogonal projection.

===== 2.4.1 Copies and a common coefficient field <copies-and-a-common-coefficient-field>

Retain the ordered Hamiltonian copies chosen in Section 2. Each of the finitely many tests $K_(ell \, M)$ occurs cofinally: after every label there is a later copy of each test. Finite sets in the exhaustion will always include the original tests and at least three copies of each test. Denote the immersed object by $L$, with its grading and spin data understood.

#thm([Lemma 2.14.])[
  (Invertibility of copy arrows).  #emph[In each finite immersed category containing at least three copies of each test, every nonzero degree-zero cohomology class between distinct copies of the same test is invertible.]
] <realization:copies>

#proof[
  The strict pair comparison gives a one-dimensional degree-zero cohomology space between copies. Given three distinct copies, choose their directed order in any desired way. Proposition 32, ordinary HMS, and Hamiltonian-copy invariance in each factor show that the product of two nonzero composable degree-zero classes between those copies is nonzero.
  Let $u : A arrow.r B$ and $v : B arrow.r A$ be such classes. Choose a third copy $C$ and a nonzero class $w : A arrow.r C$. If $v u = 0$, associativity would give $0 = w \( v u \) = \( w v \) u$, contrary to the nonzero-product assertion on distinct copies. Thus $v u eq.not 0$, and similarly $u v eq.not 0$. The self degree-zero cohomology has dimension at most one, because its canonical chain space in that degree is $H^0 \( K_(ell \, M) \)$. The cohomological units therefore make $v u$ and $u v$ nonzero multiples of the identities. Rescaling one arrow gives inverse classes. ◻
]

The ordinary mirrors of all copies can be fixed once and for all. Indeed, first choose the finite ordinary factor categories on the original tests and their specified images $Q_ell \, N_M$. For each larger finite system, compare ordinary choices after including these original labels. Hamiltonian copies are isomorphic to them in the ordinary categories. The finite comparison can consequently use the same mirror objects, with repeated labels. No analytic functor on an infinite collection is needed.

The finite cyclic categories and their comparisons need not be compatible as the label set increases. We assemble them by a field ultraproduct: the canonical complexes bound each Hom dimension, and every equation involving fixed labels is a finite matrix identity. These two facts allow all the required structures to pass to one coefficient field in the next lemma.

#thm([Lemma 2.15.])[
  (Simultaneous finite comparisons).  #emph[Over an extension field $𝕜$ of characteristic zero and cardinality at most the continuum, there is a proper uncurved cyclic $A_oo$ category containing $L$ and all the test copies. Its directed subcategory on the test copies admits an $A_oo$ functor to fixed mirror models, inducing isomorphisms on the cohomology of every strict arrow. The degree-two bound for $L$ is 
$ dim_𝕜 "Hom"^2 \( L \, L \) lt.eq b_2 \( D \) - dim cal(Z) . quad "(2.24)" $ <realization:ambient-bound>

 The category has cohomological units and a nondegenerate cyclic pairing of dimension $n$.]
] <realization:field>

#proof[
  Exhaust the labels by finite systems. For each one, take the uncurved $A_oo$ category of Proposition 29, with its bound (2.11), and the directed comparison of Proposition 32, extending its coefficient field when necessary. Pass to minimal models. The immersed models can be chosen cyclic: a finite complex with a nondegenerate compatible pairing admits a splitting and a contraction compatible with that pairing. Transfer the directed functors to these models and to the fixed mirror models on the repeated labels.
  For any fixed pair of labels, the dimensions and degrees of the minimal Hom spaces are bounded by the ranks of the corresponding finite canonical complexes. Choose a nonprincipal ultrafilter on the exhaustion. For each fixed Hom, its graded ranks are constant on a set in the ultrafilter. Use bases of those ranks and take the ordinary field ultraproduct of the coefficient fields. Every fixed $A_oo$ equation and functor equation is a finite matrix identity, so passes to this field. The same applies to units, cyclicity, nondegeneracy of the pairings, invertibility of the strict-arrow cohomology maps and of the copy arrows, and (2.24). The fixed mirror models undergo ordinary scalar extension along the diagonal embedding of their common ground field.
  The finite canonical ranks show that the resulting category is proper. All fields in the finite constructions can be taken to have cardinality at most the continuum, and a countable ultraproduct has the same bound. There is therefore an abstract embedding of the resulting field into $bb(C)$. The construction has used ordinary finite-object HMS before taking this ultraproduct; it makes no assertion of analytic mirror symmetry over an ultraproduct field. ◻
]

===== 2.4.2 Recovering full morphisms from directed copies <recovering-full-morphisms-from-directed-copies>

We explain why the directed comparison determines the enhanced full subcategory of copies. The two receiving categories are the full subcategory of test copies in the immersed category and the full subcategory of their specified mirror images, with repeated labels. Replace both receiving categories by strictly unital models, transport the functors on strict words, and adjoin formal units to the directed category. Write $cal(D)$ for this unital directed category, and let $cal(C)$ denote either full category of copies receiving it. The receiving functor is a quasi-isomorphism on strict arrows, and copy arrows become isomorphisms in cohomology by Lemma 36 and its ordinary mirror counterpart.

For a label $j$, write $h_j^(cal(D)) = "Hom"_(cal(D)) \( - \, j \)$ for its right Yoneda module. Choose successive later copies $j_0 \, j_1 \, dots.h$ of $j$ and degree-zero copy arrows between them. Restriction of the full representable admits a natural comparison

$ "hocolim"_k h_(j_k)^(cal(D)) arrow.r "Hom"_(cal(C)) \( - \, j \) \|_(cal(D)) . quad "(2.25)" $ <realization:telescope>

To construct it, first take the same telescope of restricted full representables. All its arrows are equivalences, so its homotopy colimit is the right-hand side. The Yoneda comparisons from directed representables to restricted full ones commute with the arrows up to the natural homotopies and induce (2.25). At a fixed argument label $i$, every sufficiently late $j_k$ satisfies $i < j_k$, where the comparison on Hom is a quasi-isomorphism. Thus (2.25) is a quasi-isomorphism on every argument.

Taking Hom from this telescope to another restricted full representable gives the homotopy limit of its values at $j_k$. The transition maps in that homotopy limit are equivalences, because the full-category copy arrows are invertible. It follows that restriction of Yoneda is fully faithful on the full copy category. This argument applies to higher $A_oo$ functors as well, using their natural Yoneda comparisons.

On both sides the restricted representable of each original test is therefore the same telescope in the derived module category of $cal(D)$. The enhanced split-closed triangulated hulls of the two full categories consequently agree. By Lemma 5 and sphere generation, their mirror hull is

$ cal(A) tilde.eq "Perf" \( B times E^g \) . quad "(2.26)" $ <realization:mirror-hull>

Here we use ordinary external-product generation for smooth projective varieties: external products of generators of the two factors generate the perfect category of their product.

===== 2.4.3 Projection and the Euler pairings <projection-and-the-euler-pairings>

Let $cal(F)$ be the split-closed triangulated hull of the immersed category including $L$. Its nondegenerate cyclic pairing makes it proper with Serre functor $\[ n \]$; the pairing extends to twisted complexes and then to retracts. The full subcategory $cal(A)$ in (2.26) is saturated #cite(label("BondalVanDenBergh")). The module $"Hom"_(cal(F)) \( - \, L \) \|_(cal(A))$ has finite-dimensional total cohomology at every object, by properness. Saturation therefore represents it by an object $cal(E) in cal(A)$. The representing map fits into a triangle

$ cal(E) arrow.r L arrow.r R arrow.r cal(E) \[ 1 \] \, #h(2em) "Hom"^(*) \( cal(A) \, R \) = 0 . $

Serre duality in $cal(F)$ gives $"Hom"^(*) \( R \, cal(A) \) = 0$ as well. The connecting map vanishes, and the triangle splits. In particular $cal(E)$ is a summand of $L$, so

$ dim "Ext"^2 \( cal(E) \, cal(E) \) lt.eq dim "Hom"_(cal(F))^2 \( L \, L \) lt.eq b_2 \( D \) - dim cal(Z) . $

After the abstract field embedding, this proves (1.5) on the complex mirror.

For every test object, the orthogonal summand $R$ has zero Euler pairing. The Euler pairing with $L$ is computed by its transverse Floer complex, hence equals the oriented intersection pairing with the immersed fundamental class, up to the common sign fixed by the grading convention. Equation (2.1) says $"PD" thin i_(*) \[ L \] = nu a$ with $nu > 0$. It follows that all the test Euler pairings equal the pairings with $a$ times one common nonzero rational scalar. This is (1.6) and completes the proof of Proposition 2.


